{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-918",
              "name": "Unauthenticated SSRF in 'iptv-fetch' IPC bridge with caller-controlled headers/body, no address policy and automatic redirect following",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated SSRF in 'iptv-fetch' IPC bridge with caller-controlled headers/body, no address policy and automatic redirect following"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `iptv-fetch` IPC handler in the Electron main process acts as a general-purpose server-side request proxy. It forwards a renderer-supplied URL, HTTP method, arbitrary headers and arbitrary body to `fetch()` with no scheme allowlist, no host/IP filtering and no redirect policy, then returns the full response body back to the caller. A loopback-only internal service's secret was read through the bridge and exfiltrated to an attacker-controlled endpoint in a working proof of concept."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `iptv-fetch` IPC handler in the Electron main process acts as a general-purpose server-side request proxy. It forwards a renderer-supplied URL, HTTP method, arbitrary headers and arbitrary body to `fetch()` with no scheme allowlist, no host/IP filtering and no redirect policy, then returns the full response body back to the caller. A loopback-only internal service's secret was read through the bridge and exfiltrated to an attacker-controlled endpoint in a working proof of concept.\n\nAny party that can influence a URL handed to the bridge - a hostile shared activation code (which the app stores and auto-loads on next launch), a poisoned or hijacked provider, a socially engineered EPG/playlist URL, or any script running in the renderer - gets a read/write primitive into every service the user's machine can reach: Ollama/MongoDB/Redis/admin panels on `127.0.0.1`, router and NAS admin interfaces on the LAN, and cloud instance metadata at `169.254.169.254`/`metadata.google.internal`. The response body is returned in full to the caller, and because method, headers and body are also attacker-chosen, the primitive is not read-only: the proof of concept drove an internal service with a forged `Authorization` header and an attacker-authored JSON body. Exfiltration is trivial using the same bridge to POST the stolen data to an attacker endpoint.\n\n1. Enforce a URL policy in the main process before every outbound fetch: accept only `http:` and `https:`, and reject the request unless the hostname resolves exclusively to public addresses. Block `169.254.0.0/16`, `fe80::/10`, all RFC1918 and CGNAT ranges, `100.64.0.0/10`, `0.0.0.0/8`, multicast and reserved space, `::1`, IPv6 ULA (`fc00::/7`), IPv4-mapped IPv6 equivalents, and the hostnames `localhost` and `metadata.google.internal`. Do not rely on string matching for the IP check - resolve with `dns.lookup({ all: true })` and test the resolved addresses, so decimal/octal/hex and `127.1` spellings are caught.\n2. Do not follow redirects implicitly. Fetch with `redirect: 'manual'`, re-apply the full policy to every `Location` before following it, and cap the hop count.\n3. Restrict the request metadata the caller may set. `Authorization`, `Cookie` and custom `X-` headers should not be forwarded from renderer input to arbitrary hosts; if a provider needs a specific header, whitelist the exact header names per call site.\n4. As defense-in-depth against DNS rebinding between the policy check and the actual connection, resolve the hostname once and connect to that pinned address rather than letting the HTTP client re-resolve.\n5. Apply the same policy to the remote branch of `get-stream-url`, which currently returns a loopback transcoder URL containing any `http(s)` string the renderer supplies.\n6. Separately, reconsider the renderer's `nodeIntegration: true`, `contextIsolation: false` and `webSecurity: false` posture. While the renderer holds full Node.js privileges and has no navigation guard, any renderer code execution escalates to local code execution, and hardening this bridge alone will not prevent that. A preload with a narrow, validated IPC surface and `contextIsolation: true` removes both problems at once.",
                "markdown": "The `iptv-fetch` IPC handler in the Electron main process acts as a general-purpose server-side request proxy. It forwards a renderer-supplied URL, HTTP method, arbitrary headers and arbitrary body to `fetch()` with no scheme allowlist, no host/IP filtering and no redirect policy, then returns the full response body back to the caller. A loopback-only internal service's secret was read through the bridge and exfiltrated to an attacker-controlled endpoint in a working proof of concept.\n\nAny party that can influence a URL handed to the bridge - a hostile shared activation code (which the app stores and auto-loads on next launch), a poisoned or hijacked provider, a socially engineered EPG/playlist URL, or any script running in the renderer - gets a read/write primitive into every service the user's machine can reach: Ollama/MongoDB/Redis/admin panels on `127.0.0.1`, router and NAS admin interfaces on the LAN, and cloud instance metadata at `169.254.169.254`/`metadata.google.internal`. The response body is returned in full to the caller, and because method, headers and body are also attacker-chosen, the primitive is not read-only: the proof of concept drove an internal service with a forged `Authorization` header and an attacker-authored JSON body. Exfiltration is trivial using the same bridge to POST the stolen data to an attacker endpoint.\n\n1. Enforce a URL policy in the main process before every outbound fetch: accept only `http:` and `https:`, and reject the request unless the hostname resolves exclusively to public addresses. Block `169.254.0.0/16`, `fe80::/10`, all RFC1918 and CGNAT ranges, `100.64.0.0/10`, `0.0.0.0/8`, multicast and reserved space, `::1`, IPv6 ULA (`fc00::/7`), IPv4-mapped IPv6 equivalents, and the hostnames `localhost` and `metadata.google.internal`. Do not rely on string matching for the IP check - resolve with `dns.lookup({ all: true })` and test the resolved addresses, so decimal/octal/hex and `127.1` spellings are caught.\n2. Do not follow redirects implicitly. Fetch with `redirect: 'manual'`, re-apply the full policy to every `Location` before following it, and cap the hop count.\n3. Restrict the request metadata the caller may set. `Authorization`, `Cookie` and custom `X-` headers should not be forwarded from renderer input to arbitrary hosts; if a provider needs a specific header, whitelist the exact header names per call site.\n4. As defense-in-depth against DNS rebinding between the policy check and the actual connection, resolve the hostname once and connect to that pinned address rather than letting the HTTP client re-resolve.\n5. Apply the same policy to the remote branch of `get-stream-url`, which currently returns a loopback transcoder URL containing any `http(s)` string the renderer supplies.\n6. Separately, reconsider the renderer's `nodeIntegration: true`, `contextIsolation: false` and `webSecurity: false` posture. While the renderer holds full Node.js privileges and has no navigation guard, any renderer code execution escalates to local code execution, and hardening this bridge alone will not prevent that. A preload with a narrow, validated IPC surface and `contextIsolation: true` removes both problems at once."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-22",
              "name": "Local file read via loopback /stream?path= — no containment after the stream-token check",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Local file read via loopback /stream?path= — no containment after the stream-token check"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The loopback media server's `/stream` route passes the request's `path` query parameter straight to `fs.createReadStream` with no containment check, from any absolute filesystem location. The only guard is a 32-hex-character random token that is deliberately printed to the application's console log alongside the same URL. Any other process on the machine that can read that console output (or the renderer console) can therefore read arbitrary local files — credential stores, config files, SSH keys — through the player's own HTTP endpoint."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The loopback media server's `/stream` route passes the request's `path` query parameter straight to `fs.createReadStream` with no containment check, from any absolute filesystem location. The only guard is a 32-hex-character random token that is deliberately printed to the application's console log alongside the same URL. Any other process on the machine that can read that console output (or the renderer console) can therefore read arbitrary local files — credential stores, config files, SSH keys — through the player's own HTTP endpoint.\n\nAn attacker holding only local, unprivileged read access to the application's console output obtains arbitrary file read as the user running the player. Demonstrated over HTTP against a verbatim extraction of the route: `/etc/passwd` returned in full, `main.js` source returned, and traversal-escaped paths returned, while a request without the token still returned `403`. Because the route also honours HTTP `Range`, an attacker can retrieve any byte window of any file, so prefix-based redaction or tailing only the start of a file provides no protection. On the target platform the same primitive exposes the user's browser credential stores, SSH keys, and application configuration/API keys.\n\nConfine every local path served by the media route to an explicit media-root allowlist before any filesystem call. Resolve the request value to a canonical real path, resolve each configured root the same way, and accept the request only when the canonical path is a strict descendant of one of those roots; reject on any resolution failure. Apply the containment result to every downstream use — existence check, stat, codec probe, ffmpeg input and the read stream — rather than checking and then reusing the raw parameter. Treat symlinks as escapes by canonicalising with a real-path resolution rather than lexical normalisation alone, and return the same not-found response for both \"missing\" and \"outside root\" so the endpoint cannot be used to probe for file existence. Keep the token as defence in depth, but do not rely on it for this boundary: stop writing request paths and full ffmpeg command lines to the console, and stop printing the token-bearing URL at startup.",
                "markdown": "The loopback media server's `/stream` route passes the request's `path` query parameter straight to `fs.createReadStream` with no containment check, from any absolute filesystem location. The only guard is a 32-hex-character random token that is deliberately printed to the application's console log alongside the same URL. Any other process on the machine that can read that console output (or the renderer console) can therefore read arbitrary local files — credential stores, config files, SSH keys — through the player's own HTTP endpoint.\n\nAn attacker holding only local, unprivileged read access to the application's console output obtains arbitrary file read as the user running the player. Demonstrated over HTTP against a verbatim extraction of the route: `/etc/passwd` returned in full, `main.js` source returned, and traversal-escaped paths returned, while a request without the token still returned `403`. Because the route also honours HTTP `Range`, an attacker can retrieve any byte window of any file, so prefix-based redaction or tailing only the start of a file provides no protection. On the target platform the same primitive exposes the user's browser credential stores, SSH keys, and application configuration/API keys.\n\nConfine every local path served by the media route to an explicit media-root allowlist before any filesystem call. Resolve the request value to a canonical real path, resolve each configured root the same way, and accept the request only when the canonical path is a strict descendant of one of those roots; reject on any resolution failure. Apply the containment result to every downstream use — existence check, stat, codec probe, ffmpeg input and the read stream — rather than checking and then reusing the raw parameter. Treat symlinks as escapes by canonicalising with a real-path resolution rather than lexical normalisation alone, and return the same not-found response for both \"missing\" and \"outside root\" so the endpoint cannot be used to probe for file existence. Keep the token as defence in depth, but do not rely on it for this boundary: stop writing request paths and full ffmpeg command lines to the console, and stop printing the token-bearing URL at startup."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-79",
              "name": "Hostile IPTV playlist controls image request origin via unvalidated tvg-logo in the M3U parser",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hostile IPTV playlist controls image request origin via unvalidated tvg-logo in the M3U parser"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The M3U loader copies tvg-logo, group-title and the channel name from attacker-controlled playlist text into React state with no validation. tvg-logo is bound to a live img src, so the IPTV provider chooses the URL scheme and host of image requests made by the privileged renderer."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The M3U loader copies tvg-logo, group-title and the channel name from attacker-controlled playlist text into React state with no validation. tvg-logo is bound to a live img src, so the IPTV provider chooses the URL scheme and host of image requests made by the privileged renderer.\n\nA hostile playlist forces the renderer to GET URLs of the provider's choosing (data, file, or any reachable http host) with the playlist as the only precondition. The renderer runs with nodeIntegration true and webSecurity false, so an origin it is made to load is readable by the application.\n\nValidate every playlist field at parse time, before it enters state, in handleM3ULogin.\n\n1. Resolve stream and logo values with the URL API against the playlist base and keep only http and https. Reject anything else by returning an empty string rather than storing the raw value.\n2. Require the playlist base itself to be http or https so a dangerous scheme cannot be inherited through relative resolution.\n3. Reduce channel and category labels to plain text: strip tag spans, collapse control characters and whitespace, trim, and cap the length.\n4. Drop a channel whose URL does not pass the scheme check, and stop treating any line beginning with a hash as a candidate stream URL.\n\nThe accompanying change set implements exactly this. Separately, consider removing webSecurity false and nodeIntegration from the main window so that any future rendering gap does not also grant filesystem and cross-origin read authority.",
                "markdown": "The M3U loader copies tvg-logo, group-title and the channel name from attacker-controlled playlist text into React state with no validation. tvg-logo is bound to a live img src, so the IPTV provider chooses the URL scheme and host of image requests made by the privileged renderer.\n\nA hostile playlist forces the renderer to GET URLs of the provider's choosing (data, file, or any reachable http host) with the playlist as the only precondition. The renderer runs with nodeIntegration true and webSecurity false, so an origin it is made to load is readable by the application.\n\nValidate every playlist field at parse time, before it enters state, in handleM3ULogin.\n\n1. Resolve stream and logo values with the URL API against the playlist base and keep only http and https. Reject anything else by returning an empty string rather than storing the raw value.\n2. Require the playlist base itself to be http or https so a dangerous scheme cannot be inherited through relative resolution.\n3. Reduce channel and category labels to plain text: strip tag spans, collapse control characters and whitespace, trim, and cap the length.\n4. Drop a channel whose URL does not pass the scheme check, and stop treating any line beginning with a hash as a candidate stream URL.\n\nThe accompanying change set implements exactly this. Separately, consider removing webSecurity false and nodeIntegration from the main window so that any future rendering gap does not also grant filesystem and cross-origin read authority."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-312",
              "name": "IPTV provider credentials published to public third-party store under enumerable 4-digit code",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IPTV provider credentials published to public third-party store under enumerable 4-digit code"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The \"Create Activation Code\" feature publishes the user's full IPTV login (provider URL, username and password) to a **public, unauthenticated third-party key-value store** (`keyvalue.immanuel.co`) as plain base64, under a **user-chosen 4-digit key**. Anyone can read any record back without authentication using the app key hard-coded in the client, and the entire 10,000-code keyspace can be swept in a couple of minutes. Any other Doggy Player user who ever created a sharing code therefore has their provider password exposed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The \"Create Activation Code\" feature publishes the user's full IPTV login (provider URL, username and password) to a **public, unauthenticated third-party key-value store** (`keyvalue.immanuel.co`) as plain base64, under a **user-chosen 4-digit key**. Anyone can read any record back without authentication using the app key hard-coded in the client, and the entire 10,000-code keyspace can be swept in a couple of minutes. Any other Doggy Player user who ever created a sharing code therefore has their provider password exposed.\n\nCross-user credential disclosure. An unauthenticated party who extracts the public app key from the shipped client can enumerate every code in the keyspace and recover other users' IPTV provider URL, username and **password** in cleartext. Those credentials give full control of the victim's paid IPTV subscription (streaming, account settings, and any personal data the provider exposes) and are frequently reused elsewhere. The party can also **overwrite** any code with its own credential record, so a user typing a code can be silently logged into an attacker-controlled provider account — an account-substitution attack. The publisher's own record is a plaintext-to-base64 encoding, so recovering the password requires no key material at all.\n\n1. Stop publishing provider credentials to a third-party store in a form that anyone can read. The code must not be the only thing standing between an anonymous internet user and a stored password.\n2. Remove the implicit trust in a short numeric code: a four-digit key has 10,000 values and can be walked exhaustively. Any shareable identifier must carry real entropy (for example a 128-bit random token) so that possession of the token, rather than a guessable number, is what authorizes access.\n3. Encrypt the credential payload before it leaves the device, with a key derived from a secret the sharing user transmits out of band. The storage provider must never be able to read the plaintext, since it is not a trusted component of this system.\n4. Alternatively, if the sharing feature cannot be made safe with the current infrastructure, remove the credential-publication path entirely and let recipients enter their own login.\n5. Add expiry and a revocation control so a shared credential stops being retrievable after a bounded period or on user request.\n6. Treat the stored credentials as compromised for any user who has already created a code, and require them to rotate their provider password.",
                "markdown": "The \"Create Activation Code\" feature publishes the user's full IPTV login (provider URL, username and password) to a **public, unauthenticated third-party key-value store** (`keyvalue.immanuel.co`) as plain base64, under a **user-chosen 4-digit key**. Anyone can read any record back without authentication using the app key hard-coded in the client, and the entire 10,000-code keyspace can be swept in a couple of minutes. Any other Doggy Player user who ever created a sharing code therefore has their provider password exposed.\n\nCross-user credential disclosure. An unauthenticated party who extracts the public app key from the shipped client can enumerate every code in the keyspace and recover other users' IPTV provider URL, username and **password** in cleartext. Those credentials give full control of the victim's paid IPTV subscription (streaming, account settings, and any personal data the provider exposes) and are frequently reused elsewhere. The party can also **overwrite** any code with its own credential record, so a user typing a code can be silently logged into an attacker-controlled provider account — an account-substitution attack. The publisher's own record is a plaintext-to-base64 encoding, so recovering the password requires no key material at all.\n\n1. Stop publishing provider credentials to a third-party store in a form that anyone can read. The code must not be the only thing standing between an anonymous internet user and a stored password.\n2. Remove the implicit trust in a short numeric code: a four-digit key has 10,000 values and can be walked exhaustively. Any shareable identifier must carry real entropy (for example a 128-bit random token) so that possession of the token, rather than a guessable number, is what authorizes access.\n3. Encrypt the credential payload before it leaves the device, with a key derived from a secret the sharing user transmits out of band. The storage provider must never be able to read the plaintext, since it is not a trusted component of this system.\n4. Alternatively, if the sharing feature cannot be made safe with the current infrastructure, remove the credential-publication path entirely and let recipients enter their own login.\n5. Add expiry and a revocation control so a shared credential stops being retrievable after a bounded period or on user request.\n6. Treat the stored credentials as compromised for any user who has already created a code, and require them to rotate their provider password."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-312",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/312.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-798",
              "name": "Hard-coded IPTV provider credential shipped in client bundle (ACTIVATION_CODES)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hard-coded IPTV provider credential shipped in client bundle (ACTIVATION_CODES)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A complete, ready-to-use IPTV provider credential — server URL, username and password — is hard-coded in `src/App.tsx` as the `ACTIVATION_CODES` map. Because this file is imported by the renderer entry point, the secret is compiled into every distributed build of the application and can be extracted by anyone who installs it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A complete, ready-to-use IPTV provider credential — server URL, username and password — is hard-coded in `src/App.tsx` as the `ACTIVATION_CODES` map. Because this file is imported by the renderer entry point, the secret is compiled into every distributed build of the application and can be extracted by anyone who installs it.\n\nThe provider account `hBHCQDmz` on `http://premiumtest.tr:8080` is exposed to every person who installs Doggy Player, with no reverse engineering beyond string extraction from the shipped bundle. Anyone can use that login directly against the provider's player API — streaming the subscription's channels and VOD, and reading the account's user info — or enter `6923` in the app's own Activation Code field to adopt it. The credential cannot be rotated by users because they do not own it; it belongs to the developer, so the exposure persists for every future build until the literal is removed and the password changed.\n\n1. Remove the hard-coded `ACTIVATION_CODES` entry from the renderer source and treat the embedded account as compromised — rotate its password immediately, because it has already shipped in every build.\n2. Do not embed any credential that must remain secret in client-delivered code; anything in the bundle is public by definition. If a built-in activation path is genuinely required, have the client present an opaque code to a service you control and let that service perform the provider login, so the secret never reaches the device.\n3. Add automated secret scanning (a pre-commit hook plus a CI gate over the renderer sources) to prevent credential literals from being reintroduced.\n4. Audit the rest of the shipped bundles for other embedded secrets, and review whether build tooling is excluding developer-only constants from production output.",
                "markdown": "A complete, ready-to-use IPTV provider credential — server URL, username and password — is hard-coded in `src/App.tsx` as the `ACTIVATION_CODES` map. Because this file is imported by the renderer entry point, the secret is compiled into every distributed build of the application and can be extracted by anyone who installs it.\n\nThe provider account `hBHCQDmz` on `http://premiumtest.tr:8080` is exposed to every person who installs Doggy Player, with no reverse engineering beyond string extraction from the shipped bundle. Anyone can use that login directly against the provider's player API — streaming the subscription's channels and VOD, and reading the account's user info — or enter `6923` in the app's own Activation Code field to adopt it. The credential cannot be rotated by users because they do not own it; it belongs to the developer, so the exposure persists for every future build until the literal is removed and the password changed.\n\n1. Remove the hard-coded `ACTIVATION_CODES` entry from the renderer source and treat the embedded account as compromised — rotate its password immediately, because it has already shipped in every build.\n2. Do not embed any credential that must remain secret in client-delivered code; anything in the bundle is public by definition. If a built-in activation path is genuinely required, have the client present an opaque code to a service you control and let that service perform the provider login, so the secret never reaches the device.\n3. Add automated secret scanning (a pre-commit hook plus a CI gate over the renderer sources) to prevent credential literals from being reintroduced.\n4. Audit the rest of the shipped bundles for other embedded secrets, and review whether build tooling is excluding developer-only constants from production output."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-80",
              "name": "DOM XSS in statistics window chart renderer via unencoded chart data",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOM XSS in statistics window chart renderer via unencoded chart data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The statistics window builds its chart by assigning an HTML template string to chart.innerHTML, interpolating row.date into a title attribute and into element text without encoding. The window runs with nodeIntegration true, and its CSP allows unsafe-inline."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The statistics window builds its chart by assigning an HTML template string to chart.innerHTML, interpolating row.date into a title attribute and into element text without encoding. The window runs with nodeIntegration true, and its CSP allows unsafe-inline.\n\nConfirmed: markup and attribute injection into the statistics document. An attacker-influenced value becomes a real event-handler attribute rather than text. Because the window has nodeIntegration enabled, script executed there can reach require; that step was not exercised.\n\nReplace the innerHTML string template with programmatic DOM construction so every value is assigned through a property that is not an HTML parser: set the row date and count through the title property, the tick label through textContent, the bar height through style.height, and the today marker through className. Remove the innerHTML assignment entirely.",
                "markdown": "The statistics window builds its chart by assigning an HTML template string to chart.innerHTML, interpolating row.date into a title attribute and into element text without encoding. The window runs with nodeIntegration true, and its CSP allows unsafe-inline.\n\nConfirmed: markup and attribute injection into the statistics document. An attacker-influenced value becomes a real event-handler attribute rather than text. Because the window has nodeIntegration enabled, script executed there can reach require; that step was not exercised.\n\nReplace the innerHTML string template with programmatic DOM construction so every value is assigned through a property that is not an HTML parser: set the row date and count through the title property, the tick label through textContent, the bar height through style.height, and the today marker through className. Remove the innerHTML assignment entirely."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "2.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-80",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/80.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-669",
              "name": "Main window lacks any navigation/window-open guard while running nodeIntegration:true with contextIsolation:false and webSecurity:false — any attacker-origin navigation or window.open() yields full remote code execution",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Main window lacks any navigation/window-open guard while running nodeIntegration:true with contextIsolation:false and webSecurity:false — any attacker-origin navigation or window.open() yields full remote code execution"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Electron main window is created with `nodeIntegration: true`, `contextIsolation: false` and `webSecurity: false`, and `createWindow()` registers no `will-navigate`, `will-redirect`, `setWindowOpenHandler` or `will-attach-webview` guard. Nothing constrains the main window to the application's own origin.\n\nAs a result, any content that reaches the network stack of the player can replace the application document with an attacker-controlled page that keeps full Node.js privileges. Two independent primitives were confirmed on a headless Electron 38.8.6 instance configured with the identical `webPreferences`:\n\n- Assigning `location.href` to a foreign origin navigates the top-level document; the new document executed `require('fs').readFileSync('/etc/hostname')` successfully.\n- `window.open(...)` from the application page created a second `BrowserWindow` whose effective preferences were `nodeIntegration: true, contextIsolation: false, webSecurity: false, sandbox: false`; the popup executed `require('os')` and reported the renderer's Node version.\n\nBecause this player loads user- and provider-supplied URLs (IPTV/VOD stream and playlist URLs entered into the \"Stream URL\" / \"M3U Playlist URL\" fields, and channel entries inside a provider's playlist), a URL that resolves to an HTML document rather than a media stream already supplies the attacker with a same-window navigation. The absence of the guard turns an ordinary \"play this URL\" interaction into code execution under the user's Windows account."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Electron main window is created with `nodeIntegration: true`, `contextIsolation: false` and `webSecurity: false`, and `createWindow()` registers no `will-navigate`, `will-redirect`, `setWindowOpenHandler` or `will-attach-webview` guard. Nothing constrains the main window to the application's own origin.\n\nAs a result, any content that reaches the network stack of the player can replace the application document with an attacker-controlled page that keeps full Node.js privileges. Two independent primitives were confirmed on a headless Electron 38.8.6 instance configured with the identical `webPreferences`:\n\n- Assigning `location.href` to a foreign origin navigates the top-level document; the new document executed `require('fs').readFileSync('/etc/hostname')` successfully.\n- `window.open(...)` from the application page created a second `BrowserWindow` whose effective preferences were `nodeIntegration: true, contextIsolation: false, webSecurity: false, sandbox: false`; the popup executed `require('os')` and reported the renderer's Node version.\n\nBecause this player loads user- and provider-supplied URLs (IPTV/VOD stream and playlist URLs entered into the \"Stream URL\" / \"M3U Playlist URL\" fields, and channel entries inside a provider's playlist), a URL that resolves to an HTML document rather than a media stream already supplies the attacker with a same-window navigation. The absence of the guard turns an ordinary \"play this URL\" interaction into code execution under the user's Windows account.\n\nConfirmed on a live Electron instance: a document from a foreign origin runs inside the main window and can read arbitrary local files; `window.open` spawns a second fully-privileged window even after the first is compromised. Actual consequence in this product: arbitrary code execution as the logged-in Windows user — reading all documents, credentials in the user profile and in the browser profile, the Xtream credentials the app persists in `localStorage` (`doggy_xtream_url/user/pass`), writing the updater's configuration and binaries, and persistence. The attacker's only prerequisite is that the user plays a provider- or link-supplied URL that resolves to HTML instead of media.\n\nRestore the standard Electron security posture for the main window and constrain where it may navigate.\n\n1. Change `webPreferences` to `contextIsolation: true`, `nodeIntegration: false`, `sandbox: true` and remove `webSecurity: false`. `nodeIntegration` is not required by the player's design: the renderer only uses `require('electron')` for `ipcRenderer.invoke/send/on`, which a preload can expose through `contextBridge` with a fixed, named API surface.\n2. Add an explicit navigation policy on the window: cancel `will-navigate` and `will-redirect` for any URL that is not the packaged `dist/index.html` document (in development, the dev-server origin), and return `{ action: 'deny' }` from `setWindowOpenHandler`. Sending legitimate external links to the operating system browser through `shell.openExternal` after validating the scheme is the safe alternative, and the policy must be applied to every window the application creates, including the statistics window in `telemetry.js`.\n3. Remove `webSecurity: false`. Cross-origin IPTV fetches already go through the `iptv-fetch` main-process handler, so the renderer does not need the same-origin policy relaxed; nothing in the playback path requires it once `hls.js` and the media element are served from the application origin.\n4. Verify the migration against the player's two `require('electron')` call sites for playback (`src/App.tsx:704`, `738`) and the telemetry toggle (`src/App.tsx:1178`, `1183`) before shipping, because those are the capabilities that must survive the removal of `nodeIntegration`.",
                "markdown": "The Electron main window is created with `nodeIntegration: true`, `contextIsolation: false` and `webSecurity: false`, and `createWindow()` registers no `will-navigate`, `will-redirect`, `setWindowOpenHandler` or `will-attach-webview` guard. Nothing constrains the main window to the application's own origin.\n\nAs a result, any content that reaches the network stack of the player can replace the application document with an attacker-controlled page that keeps full Node.js privileges. Two independent primitives were confirmed on a headless Electron 38.8.6 instance configured with the identical `webPreferences`:\n\n- Assigning `location.href` to a foreign origin navigates the top-level document; the new document executed `require('fs').readFileSync('/etc/hostname')` successfully.\n- `window.open(...)` from the application page created a second `BrowserWindow` whose effective preferences were `nodeIntegration: true, contextIsolation: false, webSecurity: false, sandbox: false`; the popup executed `require('os')` and reported the renderer's Node version.\n\nBecause this player loads user- and provider-supplied URLs (IPTV/VOD stream and playlist URLs entered into the \"Stream URL\" / \"M3U Playlist URL\" fields, and channel entries inside a provider's playlist), a URL that resolves to an HTML document rather than a media stream already supplies the attacker with a same-window navigation. The absence of the guard turns an ordinary \"play this URL\" interaction into code execution under the user's Windows account.\n\nConfirmed on a live Electron instance: a document from a foreign origin runs inside the main window and can read arbitrary local files; `window.open` spawns a second fully-privileged window even after the first is compromised. Actual consequence in this product: arbitrary code execution as the logged-in Windows user — reading all documents, credentials in the user profile and in the browser profile, the Xtream credentials the app persists in `localStorage` (`doggy_xtream_url/user/pass`), writing the updater's configuration and binaries, and persistence. The attacker's only prerequisite is that the user plays a provider- or link-supplied URL that resolves to HTML instead of media.\n\nRestore the standard Electron security posture for the main window and constrain where it may navigate.\n\n1. Change `webPreferences` to `contextIsolation: true`, `nodeIntegration: false`, `sandbox: true` and remove `webSecurity: false`. `nodeIntegration` is not required by the player's design: the renderer only uses `require('electron')` for `ipcRenderer.invoke/send/on`, which a preload can expose through `contextBridge` with a fixed, named API surface.\n2. Add an explicit navigation policy on the window: cancel `will-navigate` and `will-redirect` for any URL that is not the packaged `dist/index.html` document (in development, the dev-server origin), and return `{ action: 'deny' }` from `setWindowOpenHandler`. Sending legitimate external links to the operating system browser through `shell.openExternal` after validating the scheme is the safe alternative, and the policy must be applied to every window the application creates, including the statistics window in `telemetry.js`.\n3. Remove `webSecurity: false`. Cross-origin IPTV fetches already go through the `iptv-fetch` main-process handler, so the renderer does not need the same-origin policy relaxed; nothing in the playback path requires it once `hls.js` and the media element are served from the application origin.\n4. Verify the migration against the player's two `require('electron')` call sites for playback (`src/App.tsx:704`, `738`) and the telemetry toggle (`src/App.tsx:1178`, `1183`) before shipping, because those are the capabilities that must survive the removal of `nodeIntegration`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.6",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-669",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/669.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Favourites and folder sync stores per-user data in a public unauthenticated store under an identifier derived only from provider URL and username, allowing cross-user read and overwrite",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Favourites and folder sync stores per-user data in a public unauthenticated store under an identifier derived only from provider URL and username, allowing cross-user read and overwrite"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The favourites and folder sync writes each user's playlist metadata into the same public, unauthenticated key-value store used for activation codes, under an identifier derived only from the provider URL and the account username. Neither value is a secret: the provider host is visible to other users of the service, and the username is a normal account identifier. Anyone who knows or guesses the pair can compute the identical identifier and read that user's favourites, then overwrite them.\n\nThe read path is also the app's normal sync path, so injected data is consumed and re-published on the next local change. This is a separate flaw from the activation-code sharing: it involves a different record family, a different key-derivation scheme, and affects every syncing user rather than only those who chose to create a code."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The favourites and folder sync writes each user's playlist metadata into the same public, unauthenticated key-value store used for activation codes, under an identifier derived only from the provider URL and the account username. Neither value is a secret: the provider host is visible to other users of the service, and the username is a normal account identifier. Anyone who knows or guesses the pair can compute the identical identifier and read that user's favourites, then overwrite them.\n\nThe read path is also the app's normal sync path, so injected data is consumed and re-published on the next local change. This is a separate flaw from the activation-code sharing: it involves a different record family, a different key-derivation scheme, and affects every syncing user rather than only those who chose to create a code.\n\nAnyone who learns a user's provider host and username — both of which circulate in the activation-code records and in provider account identifiers — can read and rewrite that user's favourites and folder structure. Because the sync reads the remote copy on every sync cycle, injected content is applied to the victim's UI and then persisted locally on the next change. The data itself is playlist metadata (favourite identifiers, folder names), not credentials, so the confidentiality impact is limited; the integrity impact is that a third party can silently alter what the user sees and keeps, with no authentication at any point.\n\nStop deriving the cloud identifier from provider details. Mint a per-install random token (at least 128 bits) on first run, keep it in local storage, and key the sync records by that token so the identifier cannot be computed by a third party who knows the provider host and username. Refuse to apply a fetched record when the local install has never minted a token, which prevents an attacker's first-run write from being consumed. Treat the current favourites records as exposed — they contain playlist identifiers and folder names — and plan their migration to the new key scheme. The durable fix, as with the activation-code sharing, is a backend you operate that authenticates the reader and refuses writes to records the caller does not own; removing derivability alone does not stop unauthenticated writes to a leaked identifier.",
                "markdown": "The favourites and folder sync writes each user's playlist metadata into the same public, unauthenticated key-value store used for activation codes, under an identifier derived only from the provider URL and the account username. Neither value is a secret: the provider host is visible to other users of the service, and the username is a normal account identifier. Anyone who knows or guesses the pair can compute the identical identifier and read that user's favourites, then overwrite them.\n\nThe read path is also the app's normal sync path, so injected data is consumed and re-published on the next local change. This is a separate flaw from the activation-code sharing: it involves a different record family, a different key-derivation scheme, and affects every syncing user rather than only those who chose to create a code.\n\nAnyone who learns a user's provider host and username — both of which circulate in the activation-code records and in provider account identifiers — can read and rewrite that user's favourites and folder structure. Because the sync reads the remote copy on every sync cycle, injected content is applied to the victim's UI and then persisted locally on the next change. The data itself is playlist metadata (favourite identifiers, folder names), not credentials, so the confidentiality impact is limited; the integrity impact is that a third party can silently alter what the user sees and keeps, with no authentication at any point.\n\nStop deriving the cloud identifier from provider details. Mint a per-install random token (at least 128 bits) on first run, keep it in local storage, and key the sync records by that token so the identifier cannot be computed by a third party who knows the provider host and username. Refuse to apply a fetched record when the local install has never minted a token, which prevents an attacker's first-run write from being consumed. Treat the current favourites records as exposed — they contain playlist identifiers and folder names — and plan their migration to the new key scheme. The durable fix, as with the activation-code sharing, is a backend you operate that authenticates the reader and refuses writes to records the caller does not own; removing derivability alone does not stop unauthenticated writes to a leaked identifier."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
