      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated SSRF in 'iptv-fetch' IPC bridge with caller-controlled headers/body, no address policy and automatic redirect following\n\nThe `iptv-fetch` IPC handler in the Electron main process acts as a general-purpose server-side request proxy. It forwards a renderer-supplied URL, HTTP method, arbitrary headers and arbitrary body to `fetch()` with no scheme allowlist, no host/IP filtering and no redirect policy, then returns the full response body back to the caller. A loopback-only internal service's secret was read through the bridge and exfiltrated to an attacker-controlled endpoint in a working proof of concept."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "electron/main.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 2,
                  "endLine": 2,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "import { initUsageTelemetry, unregisterUsageTelemetry } from './telemetry.js';"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Add imports used by the new URL policy (dns + net)"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "electron/main.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 626,
                  "endLine": 647,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "// IPTV Fetch Bridge - Bypasses CORS and Mixed Content issues\nipcMain.handle('iptv-fetch', async (event, url, options = {}) => {\n  try {\n    const fetchOptions = {\n      method: options.method || 'GET',\n      headers: {\n        'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/120.0.0.0 Safari/537.36',\n        'Accept': 'application/json, text/plain, */*',\n        'Accept-Language': 'en-US,en;q=0.9',\n        ...options.headers\n      }\n    };\n\n    if (options.body) {\n      fetchOptions.body = options.body;\n      if (typeof options.body === 'object') {\n        fetchOptions.body = JSON.stringify(options.body);\n        fetchOptions.headers['Content-Type'] = 'application/json';\n      }\n    }\n\n    const response = await fetch(url, fetchOptions);"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Enforce an http/https + public-address URL policy and stop redirects from escaping it"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "electron/main.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 933,
                  "endLine": 950,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "ipcMain.handle('get-stream-url', async (event, filePath, transcode = false, forceVideo = false) => {\n  const port = await startStreamServer();\n  const transcodeUrl = (p, extra = '') =>\n    `http://127.0.0.1:${port}/stream?path=${encodeURIComponent(p)}&token=${streamToken}&transcode=true${extra}`;\n\n  // Remote item (IPTV/VOD). Playing it directly is the norm — it only comes\n  // through here when playback asked for a re-encode, i.e. when the picture\n  // stayed black while the audio played.\n  if (/^https?:\\/\\//i.test(filePath || '')) {"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Apply the same policy to the remote branch of get-stream-url"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "electron/main.js:627 (IPC channel `iptv-fetch`)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "electron/main.js"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 2,
                        "endLine": 2
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "import { initUsageTelemetry, unregisterUsageTelemetry } from './telemetry.js';\nimport dns from 'dns/promises';\nimport net from 'net';"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "electron/main.js"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 626,
                        "endLine": 647
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "const MAX_IPTV_REDIRECTS = 3;\n\n/** True for any address a renderer must never be able to make the app reach. */\nfunction isBlockedIptvAddress(address) {\n  const family = net.isIP(address);\n  if (family === 4) {\n    const [a, b, c] = address.split('.').map(Number);\n    if (a === 0 || a === 10 || a === 127) return true;\n    if (a === 100 && b >= 64 && b <= 127) return true;\n    if (a === 169 && b === 254) return true;\n    if (a === 172 && b >= 16 && b <= 31) return true;\n    if (a === 192 && b === 168) return true;\n    if (a === 192 && b === 0 && c === 0) return true;\n    if (a === 198 && (b === 18 || b === 19)) return true;\n    if (a >= 224) return true;\n    return false;\n  }\n  if (family === 6) {\n    const v6 = address.toLowerCase();\n    if (v6 === '::' || v6 === '::1') return true;\n    if (v6.startsWith('fe8') || v6.startsWith('fe9') || v6.startsWith('fea') || v6.startsWith('feb')) return true;\n    if (v6.startsWith('fc') || v6.startsWith('fd')) return true;\n    const mapped = v6.match(/^::ffff:(\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)$/);\n    if (mapped) return isBlockedIptvAddress(mapped[1]);\n    return false;\n  }\n  return true;\n}\n\n/** Only public http(s) URLs may be fetched on behalf of the renderer. */\nasync function assertAllowedIptvUrl(raw) {\n  let parsed;\n  try {\n    parsed = new URL(String(raw));\n  } catch {\n    throw new Error('Blocked URL: malformed');\n  }\n  if (parsed.protocol !== 'http:' && parsed.protocol !== 'https:') {\n    throw new Error(`Blocked URL: unsupported scheme ${parsed.protocol}`);\n  }\n  const host = parsed.hostname.replace(/^\\[|\\]$/g, '');\n  if (/^(localhost|.*\\.localhost|metadata\\.google\\.internal)$/i.test(host)) {\n    throw new Error('Blocked URL: loopback/metadata host');\n  }\n  let addresses;\n  try {\n    addresses = net.isIP(host) ? [{ address: host }] : await dns.lookup(host, { all: true });\n  } catch {\n    throw new Error('Blocked URL: unresolvable host');\n  }\n  if (!addresses.length) throw new Error('Blocked URL: unresolvable host');\n  for (const { address } of addresses) {\n    if (isBlockedIptvAddress(address)) throw new Error(`Blocked URL: address ${address} is not public`);\n  }\n  return parsed.href;\n}\n\n// IPTV Fetch Bridge - Bypasses CORS and Mixed Content issues\nipcMain.handle('iptv-fetch', async (event, url, options = {}) => {\n  try {\n    let target = await assertAllowedIptvUrl(url);\n    const fetchOptions = {\n      method: options.method || 'GET',\n      headers: {\n        'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/120.0.0.0 Safari/537.36',\n        'Accept': 'application/json, text/plain, */*',\n        'Accept-Language': 'en-US,en;q=0.9',\n        ...options.headers\n      },\n      redirect: 'manual'\n    };\n\n    if (options.body) {\n      fetchOptions.body = options.body;\n      if (typeof options.body === 'object') {\n        fetchOptions.body = JSON.stringify(options.body);\n        fetchOptions.headers['Content-Type'] = 'application/json';\n      }\n    }\n\n    let response;\n    for (let hop = 0; ; hop++) {\n      response = await fetch(target, fetchOptions);\n      const location = response.headers.get('location');\n      if (response.status >= 300 && response.status < 400 && location) {\n        if (hop >= MAX_IPTV_REDIRECTS) throw new Error('Blocked URL: too many redirects');\n        target = await assertAllowedIptvUrl(new URL(location, target).href);\n        continue;\n      }\n      break;\n    }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "electron/main.js"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 933,
                        "endLine": 950
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (/^https?:\\/\\//i.test(filePath || '')) {\n    await assertAllowedIptvUrl(filePath);"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Enforce a URL policy in the main process before every outbound fetch: accept only `http:` and `https:`, and reject the request unless the hostname resolves exclusively to public addresses. Block `169.254.0.0/16`, `fe80::/10`, all RFC1918 and CGNAT ranges, `100.64.0.0/10`, `0.0.0.0/8`, multicast and reserved space, `::1`, IPv6 ULA (`fc00::/7`), IPv4-mapped IPv6 equivalents, and the hostnames `localhost` and `metadata.google.internal`. Do not rely on string matching for the IP check - resolve with `dns.lookup({ all: true })` and test the resolved addresses, so decimal/octal/hex and `127.1` spellings are caught.\n2. Do not follow redirects implicitly. Fetch with `redirect: 'manual'`, re-apply the full policy to every `Location` before following it, and cap the hop count.\n3. Restrict the request metadata the caller may set. `Authorization`, `Cookie` and custom `X-` headers should not be forwarded from renderer input to arbitrary hosts; if a provider needs a specific header, whitelist the exact header names per call site.\n4. As defense-in-depth against DNS rebinding between the policy check and the actual connection, resolve the hostname once and connect to that pinned address rather than letting the HTTP client re-resolve.\n5. Apply the same policy to the remote branch of `get-stream-url`, which currently returns a loopback transcoder URL containing any `http(s)` string the renderer supplies.\n6. Separately, reconsider the renderer's `nodeIntegration: true`, `contextIsolation: false` and `webSecurity: false` posture. While the renderer holds full Node.js privileges and has no navigation guard, any renderer code execution escalates to local code execution, and hardening this bridge alone will not prevent that. A preload with a narrow, validated IPC surface and `contextIsolation: true` removes both problems at once.",
                "markdown": "1. Enforce a URL policy in the main process before every outbound fetch: accept only `http:` and `https:`, and reject the request unless the hostname resolves exclusively to public addresses. Block `169.254.0.0/16`, `fe80::/10`, all RFC1918 and CGNAT ranges, `100.64.0.0/10`, `0.0.0.0/8`, multicast and reserved space, `::1`, IPv6 ULA (`fc00::/7`), IPv4-mapped IPv6 equivalents, and the hostnames `localhost` and `metadata.google.internal`. Do not rely on string matching for the IP check - resolve with `dns.lookup({ all: true })` and test the resolved addresses, so decimal/octal/hex and `127.1` spellings are caught.\n2. Do not follow redirects implicitly. Fetch with `redirect: 'manual'`, re-apply the full policy to every `Location` before following it, and cap the hop count.\n3. Restrict the request metadata the caller may set. `Authorization`, `Cookie` and custom `X-` headers should not be forwarded from renderer input to arbitrary hosts; if a provider needs a specific header, whitelist the exact header names per call site.\n4. As defense-in-depth against DNS rebinding between the policy check and the actual connection, resolve the hostname once and connect to that pinned address rather than letting the HTTP client re-resolve.\n5. Apply the same policy to the remote branch of `get-stream-url`, which currently returns a loopback transcoder URL containing any `http(s)` string the renderer supplies.\n6. Separately, reconsider the renderer's `nodeIntegration: true`, `contextIsolation: false` and `webSecurity: false` posture. While the renderer holds full Node.js privileges and has no navigation guard, any renderer code execution escalates to local code execution, and hardening this bridge alone will not prevent that. A preload with a narrow, validated IPC surface and `contextIsolation: true` removes both problems at once."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "4529e25b6656957b6fbf12054e63acfd7ede10806ef6d782224e03ea7ae8e976"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "8e76cc1e2f109df99cd2de80962d67fea5f4a7eec1f197e83c21bce155a79ea6",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-06 21:58:31 UTC",
              "target": "Doggy Player (Electron desktop app)",
              "endpoint": "electron/main.js:627 (IPC channel `iptv-fetch`)",
              "cwe": "CWE-918",
              "impact": "Any party that can influence a URL handed to the bridge - a hostile shared activation code (which the app stores and auto-loads on next launch), a poisoned or hijacked provider, a socially engineered EPG/playlist URL, or any script running in the renderer - gets a read/write primitive into every service the user's machine can reach: Ollama/MongoDB/Redis/admin panels on `127.0.0.1`, router and NAS admin interfaces on the LAN, and cloud instance metadata at `169.254.169.254`/`metadata.google.internal`. The response body is returned in full to the caller, and because method, headers and body are also attacker-chosen, the primitive is not read-only: the proof of concept drove an internal service with a forged `Authorization` header and an attacker-authored JSON body. Exfiltration is trivial using the same bridge to POST the stolen data to an attacker endpoint.",
              "technical_analysis": "## Entry point\n\n```js title=electron/main.js startLineNumber=626 endLineNumber=628\n// IPTV Fetch Bridge - Bypasses CORS and Mixed Content issues\nipcMain.handle('iptv-fetch', async (event, url, options = {}) => {\n  try {\n```\n\nThe handler builds `fetchOptions` from `options` with no validation, then fetches `url`:\n\n```js title=electron/main.js startLineNumber=629 endLineNumber=648\n    const fetchOptions = {\n      method: options.method || 'GET',\n      headers: {\n        'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/120.0.0.0 Safari/537.36',\n        'Accept': 'application/json, text/plain, */*',\n        'Accept-Language': 'en-US,en;q=0.9',\n        ...options.headers\n      }\n    };\n\n    if (options.body) {\n      fetchOptions.body = options.body;\n      if (typeof options.body === 'object') {\n        fetchOptions.body = JSON.stringify(options.body);\n        fetchOptions.headers['Content-Type'] = 'application/json';\n      }\n    }\n\n    const response = await fetch(url, fetchOptions);\n```\n\nMissing controls, all on every reachable path:\n\n- **No scheme allowlist.** The scheme is whatever the caller supplies. (In practice Node's `fetch` rejects `file:`/`gopher:`, which the PoC confirms, so the impact is bounded to HTTP(S) and not local file read - this is the one mitigating factor and it is why the score is High rather than Critical.)\n- **No host/IP filtering.** `127.0.0.1`, `localhost`, `[-1-]`, decimal/octal/hex and IPv4-mapped IPv6 forms, RFC1918, `169.254.0.0/16` and `metadata.google.internal` are all accepted. The PoC exercised five distinct loopback spellings successfully.\n- **No redirect policy.** `fetch` defaults to `follow`, and there is no re-validation after a redirect. A `302` from any host the caller can make it request is followed straight into the loopback target.\n- **No method/header restrictions.** `options.method`, `options.headers` and `options.body` are spread into the outgoing request verbatim, so the caller controls the `Authorization`, `Cookie`, `X-...` and body of a request made by the privileged main process.\n- **Full response returned.** `response.json()` / `response.text()` is handed straight back to the caller (lines 652-657).\n\n## Reachability of the `url` argument\n\nEvery call site was traced in `src/App.tsx`. The bridge is invoked at lines 1283, 1317, 1430, 1440, 1592, 1608, 1662, 1681, 1693, 1706, 1719-1721 and 2600. The URL arguments fall into three groups:\n\n1. **Fully attacker-controlled.** `iptv-fetch` at `src/App.tsx:1283` is only the *lookup*; the decoded result at lines 1287-1293 is then used directly as the Xtream panel base URL:\n\n```jsx title=src/App.tsx startLineNumber=1284 endLineNumber=1293\n      const raw = await ipcRenderer.invoke('iptv-fetch', `https://keyvalue.immanuel.co/api/KeyVal/GetValue/${KV_APP_KEY}/${activationCode.trim()}`);\n      ...\n        const data = decodeFromKV(raw);\n        if (data && data.url && data.user && data.pass) {\n          setXtreamUrl(data.url); setXtreamUser(data.user); setXtreamPass(data.pass);\n          localStorage.setItem('doggy_xtream_url', data.url);\n```\n\n`decodeFromKV` is plain base64 plus `JSON.parse` with no validation, so `data.url` is an arbitrary string chosen by whoever wrote that key. The value is persisted to `localStorage` and, at `src/App.tsx:1560-1569`, replayed through `handleXtreamLogin(true)` on every subsequent launch:\n\n```jsx title=src/App.tsx startLineNumber=1560 endLineNumber=1569\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (iptvMode === 'xtream' && xtreamUrl && xtreamUser && xtreamPass) {\n      // If already marked as logged in, restore immediately without waiting for network\n      if (localStorage.getItem('doggy_iptv_logged') === 'true') {\n        setIsIptvLogged(true); loadFromCloud();\n      }\n      // Always refresh channel data in background\n      handleXtreamLogin(true);\n```\n\n`handleXtreamLogin` builds `${baseUrl}/player_api.php?...` (`src/App.tsx:1659-1662`) and sends it through the bridge automatically. A hostile activation code therefore yields a *persistent, launch-triggered* request to an attacker-chosen origin.\n\n2. **Provider-supplied.** The EPG URL at `src/App.tsx:2600` (`loadEpg`) is fetched through the bridge and re-fetched on mount from `localStorage` (`src/App.tsx:2616`, `2625-2627`). The value cross-checks to a provider only if the user typed it; there is no validation that it belongs to the same origin as the panel.\n\n3. **Fixed-template.** Most call sites (1592, 1681-1721) concatenate `xtreamUrl` with a constant path and `series_id` from the *response*. Response fields used this way (`series.id`, `s.id`, `s.stream_id`) can only extend the path, not change the origin - this is why a \"malicious provider\" alone cannot redirect the bridge, and it is recorded as a constraint rather than a claim.\n\n## `webSecurity: false` / `nodeIntegration: true`\n\n```js title=electron/main.js startLineNumber=573 endLineNumber=578\n    webPreferences: {\n      nodeIntegration: true,\n      contextIsolation: false,\n      webSecurity: false\n    }\n```\n\nThe renderer code itself confirms the consequence: it calls `(window as any).require('electron')` and reaches `ipcRenderer` directly, with no preload bridge and no channel allowlist. Any script executing in the renderer can therefore invoke `iptv-fetch` with any URL and read the reply. `webSecurity: false` additionally removes the same-origin gate, so even a plain `fetch` from the renderer to a LAN host has its response exposed; the IPC bridge removes the remaining mixed-content and scheme restrictions and makes the request originate from the main process. No navigation guard (`will-navigate` / `setWindowOpenHandler`) exists anywhere in `main.js`, so this posture is not compensated for elsewhere. This finding is filed against the bridge itself, since the bridge is the reusable primitive; the renderer code-execution question is tracked separately.\n\n## Related surface: `get-stream-url` and `res.redirect`\n\n`get-stream-url` (`electron/main.js:933-950`) accepts an arbitrary `https?://` string and returns a loopback transcoder URL containing it; `ffmpeg` then fetches that URL. The loopback `/stream` handler detects remote paths by regex (`electron/main.js:726`) and in the non-transcode branch performs:\n\n```js title=electron/main.js startLineNumber=806 endLineNumber=808\n      } else if (isRemote) {\n        // Nothing to re-encode — let the player fetch it straight from the source.\n        return res.redirect(videoPath);\n```\n\nThis is a reflective redirect on a loopback origin - it adds no authority beyond what the caller already had (they supplied `videoPath`), so it is not itself a vulnerability, but it means the media server will happily assume the identity of any URL the renderer names. Likewise `get-stream-url` recurses back into the main process rather than adding a check. Both inherit the same absence of URL policy documented here.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Enforce a URL policy in the main process before every outbound fetch: accept only `http:` and `https:`, and reject the request unless the hostname resolves exclusively to public addresses. Block `169.254.0.0/16`, `fe80::/10`, all RFC1918 and CGNAT ranges, `100.64.0.0/10`, `0.0.0.0/8`, multicast and reserved space, `::1`, IPv6 ULA (`fc00::/7`), IPv4-mapped IPv6 equivalents, and the hostnames `localhost` and `metadata.google.internal`. Do not rely on string matching for the IP check - resolve with `dns.lookup({ all: true })` and test the resolved addresses, so decimal/octal/hex and `127.1` spellings are caught.\n2. Do not follow redirects implicitly. Fetch with `redirect: 'manual'`, re-apply the full policy to every `Location` before following it, and cap the hop count.\n3. Restrict the request metadata the caller may set. `Authorization`, `Cookie` and custom `X-` headers should not be forwarded from renderer input to arbitrary hosts; if a provider needs a specific header, whitelist the exact header names per call site.\n4. As defense-in-depth against DNS rebinding between the policy check and the actual connection, resolve the hostname once and connect to that pinned address rather than letting the HTTP client re-resolve.\n5. Apply the same policy to the remote branch of `get-stream-url`, which currently returns a loopback transcoder URL containing any `http(s)` string the renderer supplies.\n6. Separately, reconsider the renderer's `nodeIntegration: true`, `contextIsolation: false` and `webSecurity: false` posture. While the renderer holds full Node.js privileges and has no navigation guard, any renderer code execution escalates to local code execution, and hardening this bridge alone will not prevent that. A preload with a narrow, validated IPC surface and `contextIsolation: true` removes both problems at once.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest case against this finding is that provider-response fields never reach the bridge URL directly: `series.id`, `s.stream_id` and `s.id` are substituted into path segments after a constant `baseUrl`, so a merely malicious IPTV provider cannot retarget the origin on the Xtream paths (1592, 1681-1721). That constrains one vector but leaves the activation-code path fully attacker-controlled, and that path is persisted to `localStorage` and replayed by `handleXtreamLogin(true)` on every launch. A second argument is that the renderer already holds `nodeIntegration: true`, so any renderer script can call `require('child_process')` and is not meaningfully helped by the bridge - which is why this report is filed against the bridge as a reusable, typed primitive and scored on the remote-input path, not on the assumption of prior code execution. The strongest genuine mitigation: Node's `fetch` rejects `file:` and `gopher:`, so there is no local file read and no raw-socket protocol smuggling (Redis RESP, FastCGI) through this bridge; the PoC explicitly confirms both fail. Impact is therefore read/write HTTP(S) SSRF, not file disclosure or RCE, which is why confidentiality and integrity are `H`/`H` on the demonstrated internal-service read-and-write but availability is `N`. Finally, `get-network-info` and the network-type detection are unrelated and were not treated as evidence. No input validation, address filtering, redirect re-validation or authorization check exists anywhere on the `iptv-fetch` path; both the unauthenticated and the provider-scripted call paths were exercised.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the bridge were reachable only with caller-supplied URLs but the response were not returned to the renderer, this would drop to a blind-SSRF Medium; conversely, if the renderer can be navigated to an arbitrary origin while carrying `nodeIntegration: true`, the chain becomes remote code execution and the rating rises to Critical.",
              "fix_verification": "**Gate 1 - Applicability.** Line numbers were read directly from the current checkout (`sed -n` on `electron/main.js`); `fix_before` blocks are byte-for-byte copies of lines 2, 626-647 and 940. The patch adds no unrelated changes. The patched logic was reproduced as a standalone ESM module (`iptv_fetch_bridge_patched.mjs`) carrying the identical handler body plus `assertAllowedIptvUrl`/`isBlockedIptvAddress`, and it parses and runs under Node 24.\n\n**Gate 2 - Security closure.** Re-ran every PoC vector against the patched handler: `127.0.0.1`, `localhost`, `127.1`, `2130706433`, `0x7f000001`, `[::1]`, `0.0.0.0`, `file:`, `gopher:`, `169.254.169.254`, `metadata.google.internal` and the 302-into-loopback redirect are all rejected (`FIX HOLDS: every loopback/metadata/redirect vector blocked`, 0 bypasses). The redirect loop is the key structural change: `redirect: 'manual'` plus re-running `assertAllowedIptvUrl` on each `Location` means the address check is applied to the hop actually fetched, not only to the first URL. `get-stream-url`'s remote branch is fed into the same check before the loopback transcode URL containing the attacker string is returned.\n\n**Gate 3 - Bypass review.** Deliberately re-read the diff looking for what the original reasoning missed and tested alternate malicious input classes: decimal/octal/hex IPv4 spellings (blocked via `dns.lookup` resolution to the canonical address), bracketed IPv6 and IPv4-mapped `::ffff:` (handled explicitly), the wildcard `0.0.0.0` (blocked via `a === 0`), CGNAT `100.64/10` and `198.18/15` (blocked), multicast/reserved `>=224` (blocked), and DNS-based redirects (each hop re-resolved). The one residual is DNS rebinding between the policy check and `fetch`'s own resolution, since Node offers no socket-pinning hook for global `fetch`; it is noted in `remediation_steps` as a defense-in-depth follow-up (resolve once and connect to the pinned address), not claimed as closed.\n\n**Gate 4 - Preserved behavior.** Legitimate provider traffic still passes: `https://keyvalue.immanuel.co/api/KeyVal/GetValue/...`, `http://premiumtest.tr:8080/player_api.php?...`, public EPG XML and public M3U URLs all returned `allowed`. Error semantics are unchanged for callers - rejections throw `Error` inside the existing `try`, so the renderer's current `catch` blocks (which already `console.error` and fall back) keep working.\n\n**Gate 5 - Repository checks - GAP.** No syntax/type/lint/test run was possible against the real project: the assessment environment has no `node_modules` for the app, and Electron cannot launch headless here. The patch was validated only in the standalone Node 24 reproduction above. A reviewer should run `node --check` and a smoke test of IPTV login, EPG load and `get-stream-url` for a remote item before merging.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "The Electron GUI cannot run in the analysis environment, so the handler body was extracted verbatim into a standalone Node module (`iptv_fetch_bridge.js`) with the same `fetch` logic, and driven exactly as `ipcRenderer.invoke('iptv-fetch', url, options)` would drive it.\n\n1. Start a service bound to `127.0.0.1` only, exposing `/admin/config` which returns a secret, mirroring Ollama/Redis/router-administration surfaces that trust loopback.\n2. Call the extracted handler with `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/admin/config`. The secret is returned.\n3. Repeat with `localhost`, `127.1`, `2130706433` and `0x7f000001`; all five resolve and return the secret.\n4. Call the handler with an attacker-chosen `method: 'POST'`, a forged `X-Admin-Override` and `Authorization` header, and an attacker-authored JSON body; an echoing internal endpoint confirms every element was forwarded.\n5. Point the handler at a host returning `302 Location: http://127.0.0.1:<port>/admin/config`; the redirect is followed and the secret returned.\n6. Encode the stolen secret into the URL and a POST body of a second bridge call to an attacker-controlled collector, confirming exfiltration.\n7. Control: `file:///etc/passwd` and `gopher://` are rejected by Node's `fetch`, bounding the primitive to HTTP(S).\n8. Re-run every vector against the proposed patched handler; all are blocked, and legitimate public provider URLs (`https://keyvalue.immanuel.co/...`, `http://premiumtest.tr:8080/player_api.php`) still pass.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-22",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Local file read via loopback /stream?path= — no containment after the stream-token check\n\nThe loopback media server's `/stream` route passes the request's `path` query parameter straight to `fs.createReadStream` with no containment check, from any absolute filesystem location. The only guard is a 32-hex-character random token that is deliberately printed to the application's console log alongside the same URL. Any other process on the machine that can read that console output (or the renderer console) can therefore read arbitrary local files — credential stores, config files, SSH keys — through the player's own HTTP endpoint."
          },
