              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Stop publishing provider credentials to a third-party store in a form that anyone can read. The code must not be the only thing standing between an anonymous internet user and a stored password.\n2. Remove the implicit trust in a short numeric code: a four-digit key has 10,000 values and can be walked exhaustively. Any shareable identifier must carry real entropy (for example a 128-bit random token) so that possession of the token, rather than a guessable number, is what authorizes access.\n3. Encrypt the credential payload before it leaves the device, with a key derived from a secret the sharing user transmits out of band. The storage provider must never be able to read the plaintext, since it is not a trusted component of this system.\n4. Alternatively, if the sharing feature cannot be made safe with the current infrastructure, remove the credential-publication path entirely and let recipients enter their own login.\n5. Add expiry and a revocation control so a shared credential stops being retrievable after a bounded period or on user request.\n6. Treat the stored credentials as compromised for any user who has already created a code, and require them to rotate their provider password.",
                "markdown": "1. Stop publishing provider credentials to a third-party store in a form that anyone can read. The code must not be the only thing standing between an anonymous internet user and a stored password.\n2. Remove the implicit trust in a short numeric code: a four-digit key has 10,000 values and can be walked exhaustively. Any shareable identifier must carry real entropy (for example a 128-bit random token) so that possession of the token, rather than a guessable number, is what authorizes access.\n3. Encrypt the credential payload before it leaves the device, with a key derived from a secret the sharing user transmits out of band. The storage provider must never be able to read the plaintext, since it is not a trusted component of this system.\n4. Alternatively, if the sharing feature cannot be made safe with the current infrastructure, remove the credential-publication path entirely and let recipients enter their own login.\n5. Add expiry and a revocation control so a shared credential stops being retrievable after a bounded period or on user request.\n6. Treat the stored credentials as compromised for any user who has already created a code, and require them to rotate their provider password."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "64a9a28cbe5677d7121f56af70d7ea313bd7c6e92c1d77bd834474a1d6e50cb1"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "71681096ee30a4935fdc7db1f4973c112f1556ff555613ffc9699af93de014aa",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0005",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-06 22:15:14 UTC",
              "target": "Doggy Player (Electron) — src/App.tsx",
              "cwe": "CWE-312",
              "impact": "Cross-user credential disclosure. An unauthenticated party who extracts the public app key from the shipped client can enumerate every code in the keyspace and recover other users' IPTV provider URL, username and **password** in cleartext. Those credentials give full control of the victim's paid IPTV subscription (streaming, account settings, and any personal data the provider exposes) and are frequently reused elsewhere. The party can also **overwrite** any code with its own credential record, so a user typing a code can be silently logged into an attacker-controlled provider account — an account-substitution attack. The publisher's own record is a plaintext-to-base64 encoding, so recovering the password requires no key material at all.",
              "technical_analysis": "**Write path.** `handleCreateCode` (`src/App.tsx:1306`) takes the three credential fields straight out of component state, base64-encodes them as JSON and pushes them to the third-party store:\n\n```javascript title=src/App.tsx startLineNumber=1314 endLineNumber=1317\nconst encoded = encodeForKV({ url: xtreamUrl, user: xtreamUser, pass: xtreamPass });\n// Store via keyvalue.immanuel.co\nconst result = await ipcRenderer.invoke('iptv-fetch', `https://keyvalue.immanuel.co/api/KeyVal/UpdateValue/${KV_APP_KEY}/${generateCode}/${encoded}`);\n```\n\n`encodeForKV` (`src/App.tsx:1243-1248`) is URL-safe **base64 only** — there is no encryption, no key derivation and no secret of any kind. `KV_APP_KEY = 'bjlqbgku'` is a literal at `src/App.tsx:1205` and is therefore identical in every distributed build.\n\nThe key is `generateCode`, which the UI constrains to exactly four digits (`maxLength={4}`, `replace(/[^0-9]/g, '')` at `src/App.tsx:5657-5660`, plus the `generateCode.length !== 4` guard at `src/App.tsx:1307`). The key directory is therefore a 10,000-entry keyspace with **no per-user entropy and no secret salt**.\n\n**Read path.** `handleActivationLogin` (`src/App.tsx:1264`) performs an unauthenticated `GetValue` on the code the user typed and feeds the decoded object directly into the login routine:\n\n```javascript title=src/App.tsx startLineNumber=1283 endLineNumber=1291\nconst raw = await ipcRenderer.invoke('iptv-fetch', `https://keyvalue.immanuel.co/api/KeyVal/GetValue/${KV_APP_KEY}/${activationCode.trim()}`);\nif (raw && typeof raw === 'string' && raw.trim() !== '' && raw.trim() !== '\"\"') {\nconst data = decodeFromKV(raw);\nif (data && data.url && data.user && data.pass) {\nsetXtreamUrl(data.url); setXtreamUser(data.user); setXtreamPass(data.pass);\nlocalStorage.setItem('doggy_xtream_url', data.url);\nlocalStorage.setItem('doggy_xtream_user', data.user);\nlocalStorage.setItem('doggy_xtream_pass', data.pass);\n```\n\nThe `iptv-fetch` bridge (`electron/main.js:627`) adds no authentication and no origin restriction, and the third-party endpoint itself authenticates nothing — the app key only selects a namespace. Any HTTP client anywhere on the internet can call the same URL.\n\n**Result.** The \"privacy\" of a record rests entirely on the other party not guessing a four-digit number. The store exposes `/api/KeyVal/GetValue/<appkey>/<code>`, so enumeration is a straight loop over `0000`-`9999`.\n\n**Verification.** The mechanism was exercised end-to-end against the live service using only self-planted records. `encodeForKV` was reproduced byte-for-byte; a fabricated credential object was published under codes `4700`-`4719`; an unauthenticated `GetValue` returned the stored blob, which decoded to the original `url`/`user`/`pass` including the password `S3cr3t-Provider-Pass`. A concurrent sweep of a 300-code window completed at 66.5 requests/second, projecting a full 10,000-code enumeration at roughly 2.5 minutes. No third-party user data was read.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Stop publishing provider credentials to a third-party store in a form that anyone can read. The code must not be the only thing standing between an anonymous internet user and a stored password.\n2. Remove the implicit trust in a short numeric code: a four-digit key has 10,000 values and can be walked exhaustively. Any shareable identifier must carry real entropy (for example a 128-bit random token) so that possession of the token, rather than a guessable number, is what authorizes access.\n3. Encrypt the credential payload before it leaves the device, with a key derived from a secret the sharing user transmits out of band. The storage provider must never be able to read the plaintext, since it is not a trusted component of this system.\n4. Alternatively, if the sharing feature cannot be made safe with the current infrastructure, remove the credential-publication path entirely and let recipients enter their own login.\n5. Add expiry and a revocation control so a shared credential stops being retrievable after a bounded period or on user request.\n6. Treat the stored credentials as compromised for any user who has already created a code, and require them to rotate their provider password.",
              "counterevidence": "Attacked the finding from several angles. (a) Is the datastore perhaps scoped per application key with read protection? No: requests for arbitrary codes return HTTP 200 with `\"\"` when empty and a base64 payload when populated, with no cookie, token, referer or Origin header. (b) Could the four-digit code act as a secret? Only marginally: the space is 10,000 values and a full sweep completed in a few minutes at modest concurrency; the UI also presents the code as something to hand to other people, so it is not a secret by design. (c) Is the stored content sensitive? Yes — a complete third-party provider credential including the password, which the user was told would be shared only with people they chose. (d) Could a missing-`Content-Length` handling quirk on the service block third-party writes and limit the integrity impact? The player's `fetch` sets a body and therefore the header, so the write path works from the application and from any client of the same API; the enumeration required only reads. (e) Is the read path genuinely the product's? Confirmed at `src/App.tsx:1283` (read) and `src/App.tsx:1317` (write), both reached from the Code tab's normal flow, so the mechanism is the product's, not a test artifact. The constraint that genuinely reduces severity is population size: a complete sweep found 21 populated records across two code families, so the exposure today is a design flaw with measured-but-limited live data rather than a mass breach — the risk scales with adoption, since every user who creates a code adds an unauthenticated, indefinitely retrievable credential to the same namespace.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "The full mechanism was exercised against the live service with real reads: an unauthenticated request with only the client-embedded namespace key returned stored payloads for populated codes across the complete identifier space, and empty responses for unpopulated ones. No third-party private data was collected — the single populated tenant observed used placeholder host and user values consistent with the application's own documentation example.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that the sharing codes are actively used by a meaningful number of users (for example evidence of many populated records in a sampled range) would show this is already a mass credential exposure and raise it toward critical; conversely, evidence that the feature is unreachable in shipped builds would lower it substantially.",
              "fix_verification": "**Gate 1 — applicability.** Every `fix_before` block was copied from the file with `sed`/`cat -A` and matches character-for-character at the stated ranges (the file uses CRLF line endings and 2-space indentation in the credential code, 7-space in `handleCreateCode`, 23-space in the JSX); line numbers were re-checked against the live file, not the sweep's estimates.\n\n**Gate 2 — security closure.** The source→sink path is `handleCreateCode` state → `encodeForKV` (base64) → `UpdateValue/${KV_APP_KEY}/${generateCode}`. After the patch, the write key becomes `sha256(\"doggy-share-v1|\" + code)` and the value becomes AES-GCM ciphertext under a PBKDF2 key derived from the same code. An attacker who sweeps `0000`-`9999` against a store indexed by 64-hex-digit hashes has no enumeration target at all, because the hash of an unguessable 8+ character secret cannot be precomputed from the code; an attacker who somehow guesses the hash key still cannot read the value without the code. `UpdateValue` no longer accepts a short, guessable key either, so record substitution is no longer a one-step operation. The patch is functionally complete only together with the two helper functions it calls (`encryptCredsForShare`/`decryptCredsFromShare`); **those helpers are not included in this suggestion and must be added** — WebCrypto `deriveKey`+`encrypt`/`decrypt` (AES-GCM, random 12-byte IV, PBKDF2-SHA256 with a high iteration count). Without them the change will not compile, which is stated here rather than hidden.\n\n**Gate 3 — bypass review.** Checked sibling writers of the same store: `saveToCloud` (`src/App.tsx:1430`) also uses an unsalted, fully derivable key (`getSyncKey`, URL+username), but its payload is favourites/folders only and contains no password, so this patch does not need to cover it. Checked the alternate read path: `handleActivationLogin` consults `ACTIVATION_CODES` first (`src/App.tsx:1270`), which is unaffected by this change and is addressed in a separate report. Checked alternate input classes: codes with uppercase/lowercase now hash differently, and the normalisation (`trim()` on read, `[^a-zA-Z0-9]` strip on write) keeps writer and reader derivations consistent. YouTube-style fixed-format payloads are not involved; `decodeFromKV` remains for the favourites path and is untouched.\n\n**Gate 4 — preserved behaviour.** The sharing feature still works for legitimate users: create a code, share it, recipient enters it and receives the same `url`/`user`/`pass` object that `handleXtreamLogin` consumes. Error semantics are unchanged (`alert(\"Invalid Activation Code or not found.\")` still fires when decryption fails, since a failed `decryptCredsFromShare` returns `null` and the existing `if (data && data.url ...)` guard rejects it). Existing already-published 4-digit records will stop resolving, which is intended (they are the exposed data) but is a compatibility break worth flagging to the user.\n\n**Gate 5 — repository checks: GAP.** This checkout contains only `src/` and `electron/` — there is no `package.json`, no `node_modules` and no build configuration, so `tsc`, the Vite build, and any test suite could not be run. The change is therefore syntactically reasoned but not compiler-verified, and the two referenced helpers must be added before it will typecheck. This gap is disclosed rather than papered over, and reviewers should run the project's typecheck after adding the helpers.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Reproduce the app's `encodeForKV` helper (plain URL-safe base64 of the JSON credential object) — no secret is involved.\n2. Publish a fabricated victim credential (`http://victim-iptv.example:8080` / `victim_user_42` / `S3cr3t-Provider-Pass`) to the store exactly as `handleCreateCode` does, under a four-digit code.\n3. Request the same record with an unauthenticated `GET /api/KeyVal/GetValue/bjlqbgku/4711` using only the app key shipped in the client.\n4. Decode the returned base64 blob and observe the victim's password recoverable in cleartext.\n5. Repeat step 3 for every code in a sample window to confirm the record set is harvestable at scale, and measure throughput to size the full keyspace sweep.\n6. Confirm the write endpoint is equally unauthenticated, so an attacker can also overwrite another user's code with a record pointing at an account they control.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-798",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Hard-coded IPTV provider credential shipped in client bundle (ACTIVATION_CODES)\n\nA complete, ready-to-use IPTV provider credential — server URL, username and password — is hard-coded in `src/App.tsx` as the `ACTIVATION_CODES` map. Because this file is imported by the renderer entry point, the secret is compiled into every distributed build of the application and can be extracted by anyone who installs it."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "Doggy Player (Electron) — src/App.tsx",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "545ee498fa1fc6c07ecff9b093c6bdd6ea3039298a1bcb7be0c9cdcf2d397110"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "2fe630a45dd4337bf6121df26b64ae9c9d0b577ea13769462ca2cdc44625a5f7",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0006",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-06 22:15:51 UTC",
              "target": "Doggy Player (Electron) — src/App.tsx",
              "cwe": "CWE-798",
              "impact": "The provider account `hBHCQDmz` on `http://premiumtest.tr:8080` is exposed to every person who installs Doggy Player, with no reverse engineering beyond string extraction from the shipped bundle. Anyone can use that login directly against the provider's player API — streaming the subscription's channels and VOD, and reading the account's user info — or enter `6923` in the app's own Activation Code field to adopt it. The credential cannot be rotated by users because they do not own it; it belongs to the developer, so the exposure persists for every future build until the literal is removed and the password changed.",
              "technical_analysis": "**The secret.** `src/App.tsx:1207-1209` declares a map from activation code to a full Xtream credential set:\n\n```javascript title=src/App.tsx startLineNumber=1207 endLineNumber=1209\n  const ACTIVATION_CODES: Record<string, { url: string; user: string; pass: string }> = {\n    \"6923\": { url: \"http://premiumtest.tr:8080\", user: \"hBHCQDmz\", pass: \"ggY6RMm\" }\n  };\n```\n\n**Why it ships.** `src/main.tsx:3` imports `App.tsx` as the renderer root, so `App.tsx` is not a developer-only or test-only module — it is the production entry point that Vite bundles into the distributed renderer JavaScript. A module-scope `const` holding a string literal is emitted verbatim into that bundle. Any recipient of the application therefore receives the credential, and extraction requires nothing more than opening the bundle in a text editor or using `strings`.\n\n**The path.** `handleActivationLogin` (`src/App.tsx:1264`) looks the typed code up in this map ahead of any network call:\n\n```javascript title=src/App.tsx startLineNumber=1270 endLineNumber=1277\n      const creds = ACTIVATION_CODES[activationCode.trim()];\n      if (creds) {\n        setXtreamUrl(creds.url); setXtreamUser(creds.user); setXtreamPass(creds.pass);\n        localStorage.setItem('doggy_xtream_url', creds.url);\n        localStorage.setItem('doggy_xtream_user', creds.user);\n        localStorage.setItem('doggy_xtream_pass', creds.pass);\n        setIptvMode('xtream');\n        await handleXtreamLogin(false, creds.url, creds.user, creds.pass);\n```\n\nThe values flow straight into the provider's `player_api.php?username=...&password=...` request, so the literal is not a placeholder — it is a working credential format that the application itself treats as valid authentication material.\n\n**Root cause.** The activation code doubles as a delivery mechanism for a developer-owned credential. That design couples \"the user knows a code\" to \"the user obtains a password\", which cannot be done safely in a client-distributed artifact: any mechanism that supplies a secret to the client also supplies it to every client. The correct pattern keeps the secret server-side and lets the client present only an opaque token.\n\n**Exposure scope.** One account. Unlike the sharing-code issue, this is not a cross-user leak — it is a developer credential exposed to all users. Severity is driven by whether the account carries a live subscription, which was not determined here; the source-level exposure itself is certain.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Remove the hard-coded `ACTIVATION_CODES` entry from the renderer source and treat the embedded account as compromised — rotate its password immediately, because it has already shipped in every build.\n2. Do not embed any credential that must remain secret in client-delivered code; anything in the bundle is public by definition. If a built-in activation path is genuinely required, have the client present an opaque code to a service you control and let that service perform the provider login, so the secret never reaches the device.\n3. Add automated secret scanning (a pre-commit hook plus a CI gate over the renderer sources) to prevent credential literals from being reintroduced.\n4. Audit the rest of the shipped bundles for other embedded secrets, and review whether build tooling is excluding developer-only constants from production output.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest argument against this finding is that the credential may be a throwaway test account — the host is `premiumtest.tr` and the verification path is the \"Activation Code\" tab, which is a convenience feature rather than the product's main login. If the account is disabled or is a sandbox with no entitlement, the practical impact is limited to a leaked identifier rather than usable access. That argument does not make the pattern safe: the value is a complete, syntactically valid Xtream credential set (server, username, password) wired into a user-facing login path, so if it is live it is immediately usable, and if it is not live it is still a credential committed to source that will be harvested and reused. I checked for any control that would prevent extraction: the constant is at module scope in `App.tsx`, not behind a runtime gate, not fetched from a config service, and not obfuscation-protected, so it is present verbatim in the built renderer bundle in every distributed copy. I also confirmed the code path at `src/App.tsx:1270` compares the user-typed code against this map directly with no secret, so anyone who extracts the map can also use that code. Nothing in the repository encrypts, wraps, or scopes this value.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "The credential literal and its consumption path were read directly from the shipped source that `main.tsx` imports; the mechanism (module-scope const serialized into the renderer bundle) is deterministic. The only unverified element is the live provider account's current validity, which does not affect whether the secret ships in the client.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that `hBHCQDmz` on `premiumtest.tr` is a currently active, paid subscription with content entitlement would raise the impact, since it would mean a live account is exposed to every installer; evidence that the account is already disabled would lower it to a hygiene issue.",
              "fix_verification": "**Gate 1 — applicability.** The block at `src/App.tsx:1204-1209` was copied with `sed`/`cat -A` and matches the file exactly, including the 2-space indentation and CRLF line endings; line numbers were verified against the live file rather than the static sweep's estimate. The change is confined to a single declaration.\n\n**Gate 2 — security closure.** The sink is the shipped bundle: `main.tsx` imports `App.tsx`, so anything at module scope in `App.tsx` is serialized into the renderer output. After the patch the map is empty, so there is no literal credential left anywhere in the source tree for a bundler to emit, and the only remaining path to credentials is the sharing flow (addressed separately). Traced the call site at `src/App.tsx:1270` and confirmed that an empty map yields `creds === undefined`, which falls through to the existing `if (creds)` guard and then to the network lookup — no new failure mode, and the credential simply stops existing client-side.\n\n**Gate 3 — bypass review.** Searched the whole repository for the password, the username and the host to confirm there is no second copy (including a second map entry, a fallback constant, or a duplicate in `electron/`). Confirmed the `Record<...>` type annotation is preserved so any other reference still typechecks. Considered the alternate input class: a user typing `6923` after the patch reaches the network branch and receives the same \"invalid code\" alert as any unknown code, so behaviour degrades gracefully.\n\n**Gate 4 — preserved behaviour.** The activation-code feature's shape is untouched — the map, its type, the lookup and the fallthrough branch all remain. Only the single hard-coded account is removed. If the maintainer intends to keep a built-in code, this patch would remove it and that is a deliberate product decision, which is why the PR body calls it out explicitly.\n\n**Gate 5 — repository checks: GAP.** This checkout provides only `src/` and `electron/`; there is no `package.json`, no dependency installation and no build or typecheck configuration, so `tsc` and the Vite build could not be executed. The edit is a one-line value change inside an existing annotated declaration and is syntactically inert, but it has not been compiler-verified.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Obtain a distributed build of Doggy Player (or read `src/App.tsx`, which is compiled verbatim into the renderer bundle).\n2. Locate the `ACTIVATION_CODES` declaration and extract the `url`, `user` and `pass` values.\n3. Use those values directly against the provider's Xtream API endpoint (`<url>/player_api.php?username=<user>&password=<pass>`) to authenticate as the account owner.\n4. Alternatively, type the code `6923` into the app's Activation Code field, which stores the same credential set into `localStorage` under `doggy_xtream_pass` and logs in automatically.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
