              "description": {
                "text": "Restore the standard Electron security posture for the main window and constrain where it may navigate.\n\n1. Change `webPreferences` to `contextIsolation: true`, `nodeIntegration: false`, `sandbox: true` and remove `webSecurity: false`. `nodeIntegration` is not required by the player's design: the renderer only uses `require('electron')` for `ipcRenderer.invoke/send/on`, which a preload can expose through `contextBridge` with a fixed, named API surface.\n2. Add an explicit navigation policy on the window: cancel `will-navigate` and `will-redirect` for any URL that is not the packaged `dist/index.html` document (in development, the dev-server origin), and return `{ action: 'deny' }` from `setWindowOpenHandler`. Sending legitimate external links to the operating system browser through `shell.openExternal` after validating the scheme is the safe alternative, and the policy must be applied to every window the application creates, including the statistics window in `telemetry.js`.\n3. Remove `webSecurity: false`. Cross-origin IPTV fetches already go through the `iptv-fetch` main-process handler, so the renderer does not need the same-origin policy relaxed; nothing in the playback path requires it once `hls.js` and the media element are served from the application origin.\n4. Verify the migration against the player's two `require('electron')` call sites for playback (`src/App.tsx:704`, `738`) and the telemetry toggle (`src/App.tsx:1178`, `1183`) before shipping, because those are the capabilities that must survive the removal of `nodeIntegration`.",
                "markdown": "Restore the standard Electron security posture for the main window and constrain where it may navigate.\n\n1. Change `webPreferences` to `contextIsolation: true`, `nodeIntegration: false`, `sandbox: true` and remove `webSecurity: false`. `nodeIntegration` is not required by the player's design: the renderer only uses `require('electron')` for `ipcRenderer.invoke/send/on`, which a preload can expose through `contextBridge` with a fixed, named API surface.\n2. Add an explicit navigation policy on the window: cancel `will-navigate` and `will-redirect` for any URL that is not the packaged `dist/index.html` document (in development, the dev-server origin), and return `{ action: 'deny' }` from `setWindowOpenHandler`. Sending legitimate external links to the operating system browser through `shell.openExternal` after validating the scheme is the safe alternative, and the policy must be applied to every window the application creates, including the statistics window in `telemetry.js`.\n3. Remove `webSecurity: false`. Cross-origin IPTV fetches already go through the `iptv-fetch` main-process handler, so the renderer does not need the same-origin policy relaxed; nothing in the playback path requires it once `hls.js` and the media element are served from the application origin.\n4. Verify the migration against the player's two `require('electron')` call sites for playback (`src/App.tsx:704`, `738`) and the telemetry toggle (`src/App.tsx:1178`, `1183`) before shipping, because those are the capabilities that must survive the removal of `nodeIntegration`."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "a5578c2b2e12be61707b8fca36835df229747d29f6bdc518c3e93b0c5d43395b"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.6",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "cf6d6add98f47614c58a5132d9d4025cc1e5723a11ea064057812c3a78009254",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0008",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.6,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-06 22:25:16 UTC",
              "target": "Doggy Player (Electron desktop application)",
              "cwe": "CWE-669",
              "impact": "Confirmed on a live Electron instance: a document from a foreign origin runs inside the main window and can read arbitrary local files; `window.open` spawns a second fully-privileged window even after the first is compromised. Actual consequence in this product: arbitrary code execution as the logged-in Windows user — reading all documents, credentials in the user profile and in the browser profile, the Xtream credentials the app persists in `localStorage` (`doggy_xtream_url/user/pass`), writing the updater's configuration and binaries, and persistence. The attacker's only prerequisite is that the user plays a provider- or link-supplied URL that resolves to HTML instead of media.",
              "technical_analysis": "**Root cause — one window object with two omitted guards and three weakened preferences.**\n\n`createWindow()` at `electron/main.js:568` builds the only application window:\n\n```javascript title=electron/main.js startLineNumber=574 endLineNumber=579\n    webPreferences: {\n      nodeIntegration: true,\n      contextIsolation: false,\n      webSecurity: false\n    }\n```\n\n`electron/main.js` was searched for `will-navigate`, `will-redirect`, `setWindowOpenHandler` and `will-attach-webview`; none exist. The only `webContents` listener registered in `createWindow()` is `console-message` (line 586). The window therefore accepts every navigation Electron would otherwise let through, and the renderer keeps Node privileges across the origin change — this is the classic Electron navigation-escape-to-RCE pattern.\n\n**Why the three preferences matter individually:**\n- `nodeIntegration: true` puts `require` in the page's main world; the reproduced foreign page executed `require('fs')`.\n- `contextIsolation: false` means even a future preload would share a world with page script, so no bridge-based boundary exists today.\n- `webSecurity: false` disables the same-origin policy for fetches inside the window; the reproduction read `https://example.org/` from a page on a different origin and received HTTP 200, so a foreign document can both read attacker infrastructure and, more importantly, needs no CORS cooperation to do anything it wants.\n\n**Why `window.open` matters independently of `location.href`:** with no `setWindowOpenHandler`, Electron's default is to create a child `BrowserWindow` that inherits the parent's `webPreferences`. The reproduction read back the child's effective preferences (`nodeIntegration: true`, `contextIsolation: false`, `webSecurity: false`, `sandbox: false`) and executed Node in the child. This means a navigation guard added only to the parent window would be bypassed by a single `window.open` call.\n\n**Reachability from the product's own inputs:** the renderer accepts arbitrary URLs for playback. `handleOpenUrl()` (`src/App.tsx:2188`) pushes the typed URL into the playlist, and provider-supplied channel URLs arrive the same way (`src/App.tsx:5172`, `5206`, `5249`). `videoSrc` (`src/App.tsx:586`) is then used in two places: handed to `hls.js` / set as `<video src>` for media, and sent to the `get-stream-url` IPC handler. A URL whose host returns an HTML document rather than media lands in the `<video>` element, which simply fails to decode; the same URL is a valid top-level document the moment anyone navigates to it, and the page has no other origin restriction.\n\n**Mitigation reproduced:** with `nodeIntegration:false, contextIsolation:true, sandbox:true, webSecurity:true`, a narrow preload `contextBridge` exposing `getStreamUrl/getTelemetry/setTelemetry`, plus a `will-navigate` guard and `setWindowOpenHandler` returning `{action:'deny'}`, the same harness showed: `require` is undefined in the page, the bridge works, navigation to a foreign origin was prevented (window stayed on the app URL) and both `window.open` and `<a target=\"_blank\">` were denied with only one window remaining.",
              "remediation_steps": "Restore the standard Electron security posture for the main window and constrain where it may navigate.\n\n1. Change `webPreferences` to `contextIsolation: true`, `nodeIntegration: false`, `sandbox: true` and remove `webSecurity: false`. `nodeIntegration` is not required by the player's design: the renderer only uses `require('electron')` for `ipcRenderer.invoke/send/on`, which a preload can expose through `contextBridge` with a fixed, named API surface.\n2. Add an explicit navigation policy on the window: cancel `will-navigate` and `will-redirect` for any URL that is not the packaged `dist/index.html` document (in development, the dev-server origin), and return `{ action: 'deny' }` from `setWindowOpenHandler`. Sending legitimate external links to the operating system browser through `shell.openExternal` after validating the scheme is the safe alternative, and the policy must be applied to every window the application creates, including the statistics window in `telemetry.js`.\n3. Remove `webSecurity: false`. Cross-origin IPTV fetches already go through the `iptv-fetch` main-process handler, so the renderer does not need the same-origin policy relaxed; nothing in the playback path requires it once `hls.js` and the media element are served from the application origin.\n4. Verify the migration against the player's two `require('electron')` call sites for playback (`src/App.tsx:704`, `738`) and the telemetry toggle (`src/App.tsx:1178`, `1183`) before shipping, because those are the capabilities that must survive the removal of `nodeIntegration`.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively sought the guards that would rule this out. (a) `electron/main.js` contains no `will-navigate`, `will-redirect`, `setWindowOpenHandler`, `will-attach-webview` or `shell.openExternal` call — grep across both trees returns nothing, so no cancellation exists. (b) The renderer itself contains no `location.href`, `location.assign`, `window.open`, `target=` or `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` (verified against the raw bytes of `src/App.tsx`), so the application does not navigate away on its own; the pivot must come from a URL the player is asked to load. That constrains the trigger but does not remove it, because the product's primary feature is loading third-party URLs. (c) Electron's `sandbox` is not enabled, so there is no renderer sandbox to contain the resulting process. (d) A mitigating factor considered and rejected: `contextIsolation:false` alone would be a defence-in-depth nit; combined with `nodeIntegration:true` and a missing navigation guard it is precisely the escalation. (e) The one real constraint is that the malicious URL must resolve to a document rather than a media stream; a provider that hosts their playlist UI on the same host they serve media from satisfies this trivially.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the application were changed so every playable URL is validated in the main process (scheme allowlist plus content-type check) before reaching the renderer, the practical trigger would narrow considerably and the rating would drop; conversely, a demonstration that a normal provider playlist can drive the navigation without any user click would confirm the zero-interaction worst case at the same critical rating but with lower attack complexity.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 (security closure): re-traced source → sink through the patched code. With `nodeIntegration:false` the page's main world has no `require` (reproduced: \"require blocked: require is not defined\"), so even a successful navigation to a foreign origin yields no Node capability. `contextIsolation:true` + `sandbox:true` keeps the preload's `ipcRenderer` out of page reach. `will-navigate` cancels any URL whose prefix is not the app origin (reproduced: window stayed on the app URL while the foreign URL appeared in the blocked list), and `setWindowOpenHandler` returns `{action:'deny'}` so no child window is created at all (reproduced: windowCount stayed 1 for both `window.open` and `<a target=\"_blank\">`). Gate 2 (bypass review): checked the sibling paths I could reach — `location.href` (blocked by will-navigate), `window.open` (blocked by setWindowOpenHandler), anchor `target=_blank` (blocked, routed through the same window-open handler), and `will-redirect` (needs the same handler, which the patch adds). Not closed by the patch: `webviewTag` (still defaults to false, and `will-attach-webview` is proposed only as hardening) and programmatic `loadURL` from the main process (none occurs after `createWindow`). Gate 3 (preserved behaviour): the preload bridge was exercised and returned the same shape the renderer expects from `ipcRenderer.invoke('get-stream-url', ...)`; `handleOpenUrl`/playback were not exercised inside the full application because the renderer bundle is not part of the supplied tree — this is a gap. Gate 4 (repository checks): no test suite, formatter or build configuration exists in the supplied trees, so nothing could be run; the patch is JavaScript and was syntax-checked only by loading it in Electron (the modified preload and main harness executed without error). The `fix_before` blocks are transcribed from the CRLF source and the file is CRLF throughout, so the suggestion block is line-for-line consistent. Narrowest-complete-change judgement: the preference change plus the navigation policy are both required (each alone is bypassable), and no unrelated refactor is included.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Create an isolated Electron project and install Electron (38.8.6 was used); start a local HTTP server standing in for any provider-controlled origin, serving an application page and an \"attacker\" page.\n2. Copy `createWindow()`'s `webPreferences` verbatim (`nodeIntegration:true, contextIsolation:false, webSecurity:false`) into a minimal `BrowserWindow` and load the application page.\n3. From the loaded page, evaluate `require('os').userInfo()` to confirm the renderer holds Node privileges.\n4. From the application page, assign `location.href` to the attacker origin's `/evil.html` and wait for the navigation.\n5. Read back the window URL (it changed) and evaluate `require('fs').readFileSync('/etc/hostname')` inside the new document — the foreign page executes with Node access.\n6. From the same page evaluate `window.open('.../popup.html')`; inspect `BrowserWindow.getAllWindows()` and the new window's `getLastWebPreferences()`, then evaluate `process.versions.node` in the child.\n7. Reproduce the same with the fake origin serving media-style responses; verify that no `will-navigate` listener in the real `createWindow()` would have cancelled anything (grep confirms no listener exists).\n8. As a control, repeat steps 2-6 with the fixed preferences and guards from `code_locations` and observe navigation and popups being denied.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-798",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Creator-stats gate credential is recoverable offline from hard-coded unsalted SHA-256 digests shipped in the application\n\nThe creator-statistics gate in `electron/telemetry.js` authenticates with two unsalted SHA-256 digests hard-coded in the shipped source (`GATE_USER_HASH`, `GATE_PASS_HASH`). The username digest is in the public rockyou wordlist: `sha256(\"ADMIN\")` equals `GATE_USER_HASH`, so the username is recovered in seconds from a distributed copy of the application. The password digest is the same construction and is therefore equally exposed to offline dictionary attack; it did not appear in a rockyou pass with leetspeak and trailing-digit mutations.\n\nThe credentials survive into the packaged application as readable JavaScript, so the protection is not a secret — it is a computable function of the code that was shipped. An attacker who recovers both values can open the statistics panel in any installation (Ctrl+Alt+Shift+D) and mark that installation as an owner install."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "electron/telemetry.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 196,
                  "endLine": 208,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "ipcMain.handle('creator-stats:login', (event, payload = {}) => {\n      if (!isStatsSender(event)) return { ok: false };\n      const name = String(payload.name ?? '').trim();\n      const password = String(payload.password ?? '');\n      if (!safeEqual(sha256(name), GATE_USER_HASH) || !safeEqual(sha256(password), GATE_PASS_HASH)) {\n        return { ok: false };\n      }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Authenticate the creator statistics against a service instead of a shipped digest"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "electron/telemetry.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 14,
                  "endLine": 17,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "// SHA-256 of the gate login, so the plain words are not readable in the source.\nconst GATE_USER_HASH = '835d6dc88b708bc646d6db82c853ef4182fabbd4a8de59c213f2b5ab3ae7d9be';\nconst GATE_PASS_HASH = 'cc8c3d4e532a70e3d254ddf12050c9a85952024d51fcae660791b556df2f0c9c';"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Remove the shipped credential digests"
              }
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "electron/telemetry.js"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 196,
                        "endLine": 208
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "ipcMain.handle('creator-stats:login', async (event, payload = {}) => {\n      if (!isStatsSender(event)) return { ok: false };\n      try {\n        const res = await fetch('https://api.example.invalid/creator-stats/login', {\n          method: 'POST',\n          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n          body: JSON.stringify({\n            name: String(payload.name ?? '').trim(),\n            password: String(payload.password ?? '')\n          }),\n          signal: AbortSignal.timeout(FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS)\n        });\n        if (!res.ok) return { ok: false };\n      } catch {\n        return { ok: false };\n      }\n      statsAuthed = true;\n      markOwnerInstall();\n      cancelPendingPing();\n      revealStatsWindow();\n      return { ok: true };\n    });"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "electron/telemetry.js"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 14,
                        "endLine": 17
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "// The creator statistics gate is verified by the statistics service; no\n// credential material is shipped with the application.\nconst sha256 = (v) => crypto.createHash('sha256').update(String(v ?? ''), 'utf8').digest('hex');"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Do not ship the verifier. Move creator authentication to a service that holds the credential and have `creator-stats:login` act as a thin relay that fails closed on network or HTTP error, keeping the existing `isStatsSender()` sender check and the existing success path. If an offline gate must be kept for practical reasons, it needs a slow, salted memory-hard derivation (scrypt or Argon2id) with the salt and the verifier stored outside the distributed package — for example in the OS credential store or the user's configuration — because anything compiled into the shipped bundle is public. Separately, treat the panel itself as a non-security control: it exposes only aggregated counts, so the cost of a real authentication service may not be justified.",
                "markdown": "Do not ship the verifier. Move creator authentication to a service that holds the credential and have `creator-stats:login` act as a thin relay that fails closed on network or HTTP error, keeping the existing `isStatsSender()` sender check and the existing success path. If an offline gate must be kept for practical reasons, it needs a slow, salted memory-hard derivation (scrypt or Argon2id) with the salt and the verifier stored outside the distributed package — for example in the OS credential store or the user's configuration — because anything compiled into the shipped bundle is public. Separately, treat the panel itself as a non-security control: it exposes only aggregated counts, so the cost of a real authentication service may not be justified."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "99e913bb38f7f35e804e6bdccd8a1e31d8a6e2facfe1875fb8baf2eb56cfde6a"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.0",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "79c3b7862d8dbea076a3c54ddf21054f469f4197c70b4e1f747d4badc7f6881e",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0009",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.0,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-06 22:26:04 UTC",
              "target": "Doggy Player (Electron desktop application)",
              "cwe": "CWE-798",
              "impact": "A distributed copy of the application reveals the gate username (`ADMIN`) by offline hashing, and the same unsalted-digest construction exposes the password to dictionary attack. Anyone who recovers both can open the creator-statistics window in any installation and set that installation's `owner` flag in `instance.json`, which stops it from sending the app's daily measure of usage. Scope of the impact is limited: the recovered credential protects only aggregated, non-personal daily install counts from a public counter service and an owner marker with no security role. No user data, secret, filesystem capability or IPC privilege sits behind the gate.",
              "technical_analysis": "`electron/telemetry.js:14-17` stores the gate as two SHA-256 digests:\n\n```javascript title=electron/telemetry.js startLineNumber=14 endLineNumber=17\n// SHA-256 of the gate login, so the plain words are not readable in the source.\nconst GATE_USER_HASH = '835d6dc88b708bc646d6db82c853ef4182fabbd4a8de59c213f2b5ab3ae7d9be';\nconst GATE_PASS_HASH = 'cc8c3d4e532a70e3d254ddf12050c9a85952024d51fcae660791b556df2f0c9c';\nconst sha256 = (v) => crypto.createHash('sha256').update(String(v ?? ''), 'utf8').digest('hex');\n```\n\nThe comment states the intent — \"so the plain words are not readable in the source\". That is a hash-as-obfuscation assumption, and it does not hold: a SHA-256 digest verifies itself against any candidate the attacker can generate, and `ADMIN` is high in every public wordlist. The comparison happens in the renderer-reachable handler at `electron/telemetry.js:196-208`, so the digests must be present in the shipped bundle for the check to run at all.\n\n`safeEqual` (`electron/telemetry.js:59-67`) was reviewed for a second, independent weakness. Its length-mismatch branch calls `crypto.timingSafeEqual(right, right)` purely to keep the cost uniform and then returns `false`; the equal-length branch is a genuine `timingSafeEqual`. Both branches fail closed and hash output always has a fixed length, so no comparison oracle exists — this part is correct.\n\nThe gate's protective value is correctly scoped: `isStatsSender()` compares `event.sender` identity with `statsWindow.webContents`, so the main window cannot reach the statistics, and `statsAuthed` is only ever set by a successful login. The second handler (`creator-stats:load`, line 210) additionally requires `statsAuthed`, so a reopened window resets it. The severity here is therefore bounded by what the credential protects, which is aggregate counts and an `owner` marker whose only two readers (`telemetry.js:101` and `:169`) skip the app's telemetry ping.",
              "remediation_steps": "Do not ship the verifier. Move creator authentication to a service that holds the credential and have `creator-stats:login` act as a thin relay that fails closed on network or HTTP error, keeping the existing `isStatsSender()` sender check and the existing success path. If an offline gate must be kept for practical reasons, it needs a slow, salted memory-hard derivation (scrypt or Argon2id) with the salt and the verifier stored outside the distributed package — for example in the OS credential store or the user's configuration — because anything compiled into the shipped bundle is public. Separately, treat the panel itself as a non-security control: it exposes only aggregated counts, so the cost of a real authentication service may not be justified.",
              "counterevidence": "Argued the strongest case against reporting this. (a) The gate is genuinely gated: `isStatsSender()` compares `event.sender` object identity with `statsWindow.webContents` and both handlers fail closed, so the main window cannot reach the statistics regardless of what it knows. (b) What a recovered password unlocks is only a read of aggregated daily install counts and an `owner: true` flag whose only consumers suppress the app's own telemetry ping — no file, key, IPC capability or privileged operation is behind it. That is why this is rated Low and not as an authentication bypass of the application. (c) `safeEqual` is actually correct: the length-mismatch branch returns `false` and the `timingSafeEqual(right, right)` call exists only to keep the comparison cost uniform, so there is no comparison oracle here. (d) No other hard-coded credential exists in this module — the `abacus` namespace is a counter identifier, not a secret. The finding stands as a recoverable credential, not as a bypass.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the creator-statistics gate were found to guard a privileged operation (for example if the `owner` flag came to disable update checks or unlock IPC capabilities) the recoverable credential would become a real authentication bypass and the rating would rise; conversely, removing the panel entirely removes the finding.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 (security closure): the patched `creator-stats:login` no longer decides anything locally — it forwards the submitted pair to an authentication service and gates on that service's answer, so knowing a digest in the shipped code grants nothing. Gate 2 (bypass review): the only two callers of the gate are the IPC handler and the statistics page form; both go through `ipcMain.handle('creator-stats:login')`, which is the single edited location, and `isStatsSender()` remains the sender check in front of it. Gate 3 (preserved behaviour): the renderer already treats any non-`{ok:true}` response as invalid credentials (`usage-stats.html:237`), so a service error fails closed exactly as today; the successful path keeps setting `statsAuthed` and calling `markOwnerInstall()`. Unchanged: the window creation, resize and shortcut behaviour. Gate 4 (repository checks): no runner, formatter or test suite exists in the supplied trees; the edited handler is JavaScript and has not been exercised against a live authentication service because none exists in this codebase — this is an explicit gap, and it is why the fix is proposed rather than applied.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Read `GATE_USER_HASH` and `GATE_PASS_HASH` from the shipped `electron/telemetry.js` (they are present in `app.asar` in a distributed build).\n2. Compute `sha256(b'ADMIN')` and compare it with `GATE_USER_HASH` — the values match, recovering the gate username.\n3. Run the password digest against a wordlist with case, leetspeak and trailing-digit mutations to show the same offline attack applies to the password.\n4. With the recovered pair, press `Ctrl+Alt+Shift+D` in a running installation and submit the credentials; the response is `{ok: true}` and the window expands to show the panel.\n5. Confirm the effect persisted by reading `instance.json` in the application's user-data directory: `owner` is now `true`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "ruleIndex": 7,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Favourites and folder sync stores per-user data in a public unauthenticated store under an identifier derived only from provider URL and username, allowing cross-user read and overwrite\n\nThe favourites and folder sync writes each user's playlist metadata into the same public, unauthenticated key-value store used for activation codes, under an identifier derived only from the provider URL and the account username. Neither value is a secret: the provider host is visible to other users of the service, and the username is a normal account identifier. Anyone who knows or guesses the pair can compute the identical identifier and read that user's favourites, then overwrite them.\n\nThe read path is also the app's normal sync path, so injected data is consumed and re-published on the next local change. This is a separate flaw from the activation-code sharing: it involves a different record family, a different key-derivation scheme, and affects every syncing user rather than only those who chose to create a code."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/App.tsx"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1418,
                  "endLine": 1422,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "const getSyncKey = () => {"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Replace the derivable sync identifier with a per-install random token"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/App.tsx"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1437,
                  "endLine": 1440,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "const { ipcRenderer } = (window as any).require('electron');"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Do not consume a record for a token this install has never minted"
              }
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/App.tsx"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 1418,
                        "endLine": 1422
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "// The sync identifier must not be derivable from public provider details.\n  // A per-install random token is minted once and kept in local storage.\n  const getSyncKey = () => {\n    let token = localStorage.getItem('doggy_sync_token');\n    if (!token) {\n      const bytes = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));\n      token = Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\n      localStorage.setItem('doggy_sync_token', token);\n    }\n    const mainIdentity = xtreamUrl ? `${xtreamUrl}|${xtreamUser}` : m3uUrl;\n    if (!mainIdentity) return null;\n    return `favs_${token}`;\n  };"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/App.tsx"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 1437,
                        "endLine": 1440
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "const { ipcRenderer } = (window as any).require('electron');\n    try {\n      if (!localStorage.getItem('doggy_sync_token')) return;\n      const b64 = await ipcRenderer.invoke('iptv-fetch', `https://keyvalue.immanuel.co/api/KeyVal/GetValue/${KV_APP_KEY}/${key}`);"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Stop deriving the cloud identifier from provider details. Mint a per-install random token (at least 128 bits) on first run, keep it in local storage, and key the sync records by that token so the identifier cannot be computed by a third party who knows the provider host and username. Refuse to apply a fetched record when the local install has never minted a token, which prevents an attacker's first-run write from being consumed. Treat the current favourites records as exposed — they contain playlist identifiers and folder names — and plan their migration to the new key scheme. The durable fix, as with the activation-code sharing, is a backend you operate that authenticates the reader and refuses writes to records the caller does not own; removing derivability alone does not stop unauthenticated writes to a leaked identifier.",
                "markdown": "Stop deriving the cloud identifier from provider details. Mint a per-install random token (at least 128 bits) on first run, keep it in local storage, and key the sync records by that token so the identifier cannot be computed by a third party who knows the provider host and username. Refuse to apply a fetched record when the local install has never minted a token, which prevents an attacker's first-run write from being consumed. Treat the current favourites records as exposed — they contain playlist identifiers and folder names — and plan their migration to the new key scheme. The durable fix, as with the activation-code sharing, is a backend you operate that authenticates the reader and refuses writes to records the caller does not own; removing derivability alone does not stop unauthenticated writes to a leaked identifier."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "aadb7bf221f30cf3325eadd08e0187855bf66294948440da29367ed6917d402e"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.2",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "037363f54992fac6d3716e5778b4830f6c0a1c54def858c700fb845f5f77ff2c",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.2,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-06 22:42:49 UTC",
              "target": "Doggy Player (Electron desktop application)",
              "cwe": "CWE-639",
              "impact": "Anyone who learns a user's provider host and username — both of which circulate in the activation-code records and in provider account identifiers — can read and rewrite that user's favourites and folder structure. Because the sync reads the remote copy on every sync cycle, injected content is applied to the victim's UI and then persisted locally on the next change. The data itself is playlist metadata (favourite identifiers, folder names), not credentials, so the confidentiality impact is limited; the integrity impact is that a third party can silently alter what the user sees and keeps, with no authentication at any point.",
              "technical_analysis": "The favourites/folder sync uses the same public store as the activation codes but a completely different key scheme, and one that is weaker still.\n\n`getSyncKey()` (`src/App.tsx:1418`) concatenates the provider URL and username, base64-encodes the pair and strips non-alphanumerics. The result is `favs_` plus a deterministic encoding of public data. There is no salt, no per-user random value and no machine binding, so the identifier is reproducible by anyone who knows the two inputs — and a username is the least secret part of a provider account.\n\n`saveToCloud()` (`src/App.tsx:1424`) publishes `{items, folders}` under that identifier through `UpdateValue`, and `loadFromCloud()` (`src/App.tsx:1434`) fetches the same identifier through `GetValue` and assigns `data.items` / `data.folders` straight into component state. The service authenticates neither direction, which was confirmed live: an arbitrary identifier returns HTTP 200 with `\"\"` when absent and the payload when present, with no header, cookie or referer involved.\n\nThe loop closes at the sync effect (`src/App.tsx:1450`): whenever favourites or folders change, `saveToCloud` republishes the current state. After a tampered read is applied via `setFavorites()`/`setFavoriteFolders()`, the victim's next change writes the injected content back to the store, so the tampered state persists across reads rather than being corrected.\n\nThis is distinct from the activation-code finding: different record family (`{items, folders}` rather than `{url, user, pass}`), different key derivation (public pair rather than a four-digit code) and a different affected population (every user who syncs, not only those who deliberately published a code).",
              "remediation_steps": "Stop deriving the cloud identifier from provider details. Mint a per-install random token (at least 128 bits) on first run, keep it in local storage, and key the sync records by that token so the identifier cannot be computed by a third party who knows the provider host and username. Refuse to apply a fetched record when the local install has never minted a token, which prevents an attacker's first-run write from being consumed. Treat the current favourites records as exposed — they contain playlist identifiers and folder names — and plan their migration to the new key scheme. The durable fix, as with the activation-code sharing, is a backend you operate that authenticates the reader and refuses writes to records the caller does not own; removing derivability alone does not stop unauthenticated writes to a leaked identifier.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively looked for controls that would rule this out. (a) Is the key secret? No: `getSyncKey()` (`src/App.tsx:1418-1422`) is a pure function of `xtreamUrl` and `xtreamUser` with no user secret, and the harness shows a stranger reproducing the owner's key from those two values alone; the only variation is which username is used, so it is guessing-resistant only by the provider's own username format. (b) Is there any read authorization on the store? No — confirmed against the live service, an arbitrary-key `GetValue` returns 200 with the payload and needs no header. (c) Does the sync overwrite the server copy before reading it, so an attacker's injected data is actually consumed? Yes: `loadFromCloud` (`src/App.tsx:1434`) only reads, but the sync effect (`src/App.tsx:1450`) calls `saveToCloud` on favourites/folder changes, so a legitimate `setFavorites()` from a tampered read is written back under the same key on the next change, completing the loop. (d) Is the impact limited to playlist metadata? Yes — the payload is `{items, folders}`, i.e. favourite identifiers and folder names, with no credential and no path outside the favourites UI, which is why this is rated low rather than high. (e) What genuinely reduces confidence is population: the identifier is derived from a pair the attacker must know, and I did not locate a live record to confirm how many exist, so the exposure is real in mechanism but unquantified in practice.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "The key derivation, the unauthenticated read/write endpoints and the write-before-read ordering in `loadFromCloud` were all confirmed in the shipped source and reproduced in a Node harness; the service's lack of reader authorization was confirmed live against the same namespace on a different record family. The gap is that no live favourites record was located or modified: the derivation requires both the provider host and the account username, and the population of this record family was not established. A populated record's existence is therefore inferred from the automatic sync behaviour rather than observed.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that a populated favourites record exists for a live user (demonstrating the derivation lands on real data) would raise confidence and impact; evidence that the sync feature is disabled in shipped builds would drop this to informational.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 (security closure): after the patch the record identifier is `favs_<random per-install token>` where the token is minted once and persisted locally, so it cannot be derived from the provider URL or username; a stranger who knows both cannot compute the key that `UpdateValue`/`GetValue` are invoked with (the trace ends at the same `ipcRenderer.invoke` sink with a value the attacker cannot produce). `loadFromCloud` additionally refuses to apply a payload when the locally stored token is absent, so a first-run attacker write is not consumed. Gate 2 (bypass review): checked the other writer that touches favourites (`saveToCloud` callers) — both read the key from the same `getSyncKey()`, so the single change covers write and read; checked the m3u branch (`mainIdentity = m3uUrl`) and gave it the same token treatment; confirmed the envelope `{items, folders}` shape is unchanged so the `decodeFromKV` consumer still works. Gate 3 (preserved behaviour): the sync effect at `src/App.tsx:1450` still triggers on favourites/folder changes and the payload is still applied to `setFavorites`/`setFavoriteFolders`; the only behavioural difference is that an existing install needs one successful save before a fresh key is used, which the code handles by minting the token on first call. Gate 4 (repository checks): the supplied trees contain no `package.json`, bundler configuration or test runner, so no build, typecheck or test could be executed — this is an explicit gap. The change is a self-contained helper plus three call sites and was syntax-checked only by reasoning about the existing types.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Read `getSyncKey()` and confirm it is built only from `xtreamUrl` and `xtreamUser`, with no secret component.\n2. Reproduce the derivation for a target provider host and username; the resulting `favs_<base64>` string matches the owner's identifier exactly.\n3. Request `https://keyvalue.immanuel.co/api/KeyVal/GetValue/<namespace>/<derived key>` with no authentication to retrieve the stored `{items, folders}` payload.\n4. Write a modified payload to `https://keyvalue.immanuel.co/api/KeyVal/UpdateValue/<namespace>/<derived key>/<base64>`, which the service accepts without credentials.\n5. Confirm from the source that `loadFromCloud()` applies the fetched `items` and `folders` directly to component state, so the injected payload is consumed on the victim's next sync and re-published on the next favourites change.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/open-redirect",
